INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_21',
  'What is the primary benefit of using AWS Transit Gateway?',
  'mcq',
  '["To establish direct network connections between two VPCs","To enable private connectivity to AWS services from a VPC","To centralize network connectivity for multiple VPCs and on-premises networks","To provide public internet access for instances in a private subnet"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (To centralize network connectivity for multiple VPCs and on-premises networks) is the primary benefit of AWS Transit Gateway. It acts as a network hub, simplifying network architecture by connecting VPCs, AWS accounts, and on-premises networks through a single gateway. Option A (To establish direct network connections between two VPCs) describes VPC peering. Option B (To enable private connectivity to AWS services from a VPC) describes VPC Endpoints. Option D (To provide public internet access for instances in a private subnet) describes a NAT Gateway.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_22',
  'Which AWS networking feature allows two Amazon VPCs to communicate with each other using private IP addresses, as if they are on the same network?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","VPC peering","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (VPC peering) is the correct feature. VPC peering connection allows you to route traffic between two VPCs using private IP addresses. Instances in either VPC can communicate with each other as if they are within the same network. Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) is a hub for connecting many VPCs and on-premises networks, but VPC peering is for direct two-VPC communication. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) connects on-premises networks to AWS. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) connects on-premises networks to a VPC over the internet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_23',
  'A company has multiple AWS accounts and VPCs in the same AWS Region. They need to share a common set of network services, such as firewalls and intrusion detection systems, across all VPCs. Which AWS service is best suited for this hub-and-spoke architecture?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53","AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Transit Gateway) is best suited for this scenario. It enables a hub-and-spoke network topology where a central Transit Gateway connects to multiple VPCs (spokes). This allows for centralized network services, simplified routing, and inter-VPC communication. Option A (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service. Option C (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network. Option D (AWS Global Accelerator) improves application performance for global users, but none of these are designed for centralized VPC connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_24',
  'When connecting two VPCs within the same AWS account and Region, which method offers the simplest and most direct private connectivity for a small number of connections?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect","AWS Transit Gateway","VPC peering","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (VPC peering) offers the simplest and most direct private connectivity for connecting two VPCs within the same AWS account and Region, especially for a small number of connections. It allows instances to communicate using private IP addresses. Option A (AWS Direct Connect) is for on-premises to AWS connectivity. Option B (AWS Transit Gateway) is more suitable for complex, many-to-many VPC connections or hybrid cloud setups. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) connects on-premises networks to a VPC over the internet, adding encryption overhead not needed for intra-AWS private communication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_25',
  'What is the primary use case for AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)?',
  'mcq',
  '["To manage IAM user permissions across multiple accounts","To share AWS resources securely across AWS accounts","To automate resource provisioning and de-provisioning","To monitor resource configurations for compliance"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To share AWS resources securely across AWS accounts) is the primary use case for AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM). RAM allows you to share supported AWS resources, such as VPC subnets, Transit Gateways, and Route 53 Resolver rules, with other AWS accounts or within your AWS Organization. Option A (To manage IAM user permissions across multiple accounts) is handled by AWS Organizations and IAM. Option C (To automate resource provisioning and de-provisioning) is typically done with AWS CloudFormation or AWS Service Catalog. Option D (To monitor resource configurations for compliance) is a function of AWS Config.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_26',
  'A company needs to connect 15 VPCs across three different AWS accounts in a single AWS Region. They want to simplify routing and reduce the number of individual peering connections. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC peering","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) is the most suitable service for this scenario. With 15 VPCs, using individual VPC peering connections would result in a complex mesh network (N*(N-1)/2 connections). Transit Gateway simplifies this by acting as a central hub, reducing the number of connections and simplifying routing. Option A (VPC peering) is suitable for a small number of VPCs but becomes unmanageable with many. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) is for on-premises to AWS connectivity. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) is also for on-premises to AWS connectivity over the internet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_27',
  'What is the primary purpose of AWS Transit Gateway?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide a dedicated network connection from on-premises to AWS.","To enable private connectivity to AWS services from within a VPC.","To connect thousands of Amazon VPCs and on-premises networks as a hub.","To distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes the primary purpose of AWS Transit Gateway, which acts as a network hub to connect thousands of Amazon VPCs and on-premises networks. This simplifies network management and creates a hub-and-spoke topology. Option A describes AWS Direct Connect. Option B describes VPC Endpoints. Option D describes Elastic Load Balancing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_28',
  'A large organization has multiple VPCs across several AWS accounts and needs to establish a scalable and centralized network connectivity model, allowing all VPCs to communicate with each other and with on-premises networks through a single gateway. Which AWS service should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS VPN CloudHub","Direct Connect Gateway"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Transit Gateway. It is designed for exactly this scenario, acting as a central hub that connects multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, simplifying network architecture and enabling a hub-and-spoke model. Option A, VPC Peering, connects only two VPCs at a time and does not scale well for many-to-many connectivity. Option C, AWS VPN CloudHub, is for connecting multiple VPNs but lacks the full routing and scalability features of Transit Gateway for VPCs. Option D, Direct Connect Gateway, is for connecting Direct Connect to multiple VPCs, but Transit Gateway is the broader solution for VPC-to-VPC and hybrid connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_29',
  'A developer needs to establish a low-latency, high-bandwidth network connection between two Amazon VPCs located in the same AWS Region and owned by the same AWS account. The connection should use private IP addresses. Which networking construct is the MOST suitable for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","VPC Peering","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","Internet Gateway"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is VPC Peering. For connecting two VPCs in the same region (or different regions) using private IP addresses with low latency and high bandwidth, VPC peering is the most direct and suitable solution. Option A, AWS Transit Gateway, is a more complex and scalable solution for many-to-many VPC connections, which would be overkill for just two VPCs. Option C, AWS Site-to-Site VPN, establishes a secure connection over the public internet, which typically has higher latency and lower bandwidth than peering. Option D, Internet Gateway, connects a VPC to the internet and does not facilitate private VPC-to-VPC communication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_30',
  'Which AWS service allows you to securely share your AWS resources, such as Transit Gateways, across multiple AWS accounts?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Organizations","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","AWS IAM","AWS Control Tower"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM). AWS RAM is a service that enables you to easily and securely share your AWS resources with any AWS account or within your AWS Organization. This is commonly used for sharing Transit Gateways, subnets, and other resources. Option A, AWS Organizations, helps you centrally manage multiple AWS accounts but doesn''t directly share resources. Option C, AWS IAM, manages access permissions but not resource sharing itself. Option D, AWS Control Tower, sets up and governs a secure multi-account AWS environment but doesn''t directly share resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_31',
  'A company has a multi-account AWS environment and wants to share a single AWS Transit Gateway across several development VPCs located in different accounts. Which AWS service should be used to facilitate this resource sharing securely?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS PrivateLink","VPC Endpoints","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","AWS Organizations"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM). AWS RAM is designed to allow you to securely share your AWS resources, including AWS Transit Gateways, subnets, and other resources, with other AWS accounts or within your AWS Organization. This is the correct service for sharing a Transit Gateway across multiple accounts. Option A, AWS PrivateLink, provides private connectivity to services hosted on AWS. Option B, VPC Endpoints, allows private access to AWS services from within a VPC. Option D, AWS Organizations, helps you centrally manage multiple AWS accounts but doesn''t directly share resources like Transit Gateway.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_32',
  'Which type of attachment allows an AWS Transit Gateway to connect to an AWS Direct Connect Gateway?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC attachment","VPN attachment","Connect attachment","Direct Connect Gateway attachment"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is a Direct Connect Gateway attachment. AWS Transit Gateway can attach to a Direct Connect Gateway, enabling on-premises networks connected via Direct Connect to route traffic through the Transit Gateway to multiple VPCs. Option A, VPC attachment, connects a VPC to the Transit Gateway. Option B, VPN attachment, connects a VPN connection to the Transit Gateway. Option C, Connect attachment, is used for third-party network virtual appliances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_33',
  'A large organization has a hub-and-spoke network topology in AWS, with dozens of VPCs across multiple AWS accounts and Regions. They need a scalable and centralized way to manage routing between these VPCs and to their on-premises network, while also supporting network inspection and segmentation. Which AWS networking service is best suited for this complex scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC Peering connections between all VPCs.","AWS Transit Gateway.","AWS Direct Connect Gateway.","AWS VPN CloudHub."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Transit Gateway is designed for exactly this type of complex, multi-VPC, multi-account, and multi-Region networking. It acts as a central hub, simplifying routing by connecting thousands of VPCs and on-premises networks. It supports network inspection (via Gateway Load Balancer endpoints) and segmentation, making it ideal for a hub-and-spoke model. Option A (incorrect) VPC Peering connections are suitable for connecting a small number of VPCs, but they do not scale well for dozens of VPCs due to the ''n-squared'' problem, and they don''t provide centralized routing or support for on-premises connections in a hub-and-spoke manner. Option C (incorrect) AWS Direct Connect Gateway connects Direct Connect connections to multiple VPCs across accounts/Regions, but it''s focused on hybrid connectivity, not on centralizing routing between many VPCs within AWS. Option D (incorrect) AWS VPN CloudHub is a legacy solution for connecting multiple on-premises VPNs to a single VPC, and it does not offer the scalability or advanced features of Transit Gateway for multi-VPC and multi-account scenarios.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_34',
  'A company has two VPCs in the same AWS Region that need to communicate with each other using private IP addresses. The communication must be highly available and low-latency, without requiring a VPN connection or a Transit Gateway. Which AWS networking feature should a developer implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) VPC Peering allows you to connect two VPCs in the same AWS Region (or different Regions) so that they can communicate with each other using private IP addresses as if they are on the same network. It provides a direct, low-latency, and highly available connection without requiring additional gateways, VPNs, or public internet traversal. Option A (incorrect) AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private connection from on-premises to AWS, not between two VPCs within AWS. Option B (incorrect) AWS Site-to-Site VPN creates an encrypted connection over the public internet, which adds latency and is not ideal for intra-AWS VPC communication when a private connection is available. Option D (incorrect) AWS PrivateLink allows consumers in different VPCs to privately access services hosted in another VPC without traversing the public internet, but it''s for service consumption, not general VPC-to-VPC connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_35',
  'A software vendor hosts a SaaS application in an AWS VPC and wants to offer private access to their customers'' VPCs without exposing the service to the public internet or requiring VPC peering connections. The solution must simplify network management for both the vendor and the customers. Which AWS service is designed for this specific use case?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Client VPN"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) AWS PrivateLink enables you to privately connect your VPCs to services hosted by AWS or by other AWS accounts (VPC endpoint services) without using the public internet or requiring VPC peering. It simplifies network architecture by eliminating the need for complex firewall rules, route tables, or VPC peering connections, making it ideal for SaaS providers to offer private access to their services. Option A (incorrect) AWS Transit Gateway is used for centralizing routing between many VPCs and on-premises networks, but it''s not specifically designed for private service consumption across different accounts without peering. Option B (incorrect) Amazon VPC Peering connects two VPCs, but it requires managing routing tables in both VPCs and can become complex with many connections. It also doesn''t isolate the service traffic as cleanly as PrivateLink. Option D (incorrect) AWS Client VPN allows individual users to securely access AWS resources, not for private service consumption between VPCs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_36',
  'A global company operates applications in multiple AWS Regions and needs to establish cross-Region connectivity between VPCs for disaster recovery and data replication. The solution must be highly available, secure, and offer a managed service approach to minimize operational overhead. Which AWS service should a developer leverage for this cross-Region connectivity?',
  'mcq',
  '["Multiple AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections between VPCs in different Regions.","AWS Direct Connect between each Region.","AWS Transit Gateway with inter-Region peering.","VPC Peering connections between all relevant VPCs across Regions."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) AWS Transit Gateway with inter-Region peering allows you to connect Transit Gateways in different AWS Regions, enabling global network connectivity for your VPCs and on-premises networks. This provides a highly available, secure, and managed solution for cross-Region communication, simplifying routing and minimizing operational overhead compared to managing individual connections. Option A (incorrect) while technically possible, managing multiple AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections between VPCs in different Regions would be complex, incur higher operational overhead, and might not offer the same performance or scalability as Transit Gateway. Option B (incorrect) AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private connection from on-premises to an AWS Region, not directly between two AWS Regions. Option D (incorrect) VPC Peering connections can connect VPCs across Regions, but they do not scale well for complex scenarios with many VPCs and do not offer the centralized routing and management capabilities of Transit Gateway for a global network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_37',
  'A company has several development and production VPCs in separate AWS accounts. They need to share common network resources, such as a centralized NAT Gateway or a set of security appliances, across these VPCs without duplicating them in each account. Which AWS service enables this resource sharing efficiently and securely?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Organizations for consolidated billing.","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).","VPC Peering connections between all VPCs.","AWS PrivateLink for private service access."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) allows you to securely share your AWS resources with other AWS accounts or within your AWS Organization. This is ideal for sharing centralized network components like Transit Gateways, Subnets (for NAT Gateways or security appliances), and Route 53 Resolver rules, avoiding duplication and simplifying management across multiple VPCs and accounts. Option A (incorrect) AWS Organizations helps manage multiple AWS accounts and consolidate billing, but it doesn''t directly provide the mechanism for sharing network resources between VPCs. Option C (incorrect) VPC Peering connects two VPCs, but it doesn''t allow for sharing a single instance of a resource (like a NAT Gateway) across multiple peered VPCs without additional routing complexity. Option D (incorrect) AWS PrivateLink enables private access to services hosted in another VPC, but it''s for service consumption, not for sharing underlying network infrastructure components like NAT Gateways or subnets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_38',
  'A large enterprise is consolidating its network architecture in AWS and wants to implement a centralized network inspection VPC. All traffic between application VPCs and the internet, as well as between different application VPCs, must pass through this inspection VPC for security filtering and logging. Which AWS service is crucial for directing traffic through a centralized inspection VPC in a scalable manner?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon VPC Peering to connect all application VPCs to the inspection VPC.","AWS Transit Gateway with attachment to the inspection VPC and route table configurations.","AWS Network Firewall deployed in each application VPC.","AWS Direct Connect Gateway to route traffic to the inspection VPC."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Transit Gateway is the ideal solution for centralizing network inspection. All application VPCs can attach to the Transit Gateway, and the inspection VPC can also attach to it. By configuring Transit Gateway route tables, traffic can be steered from application VPCs to the inspection VPC (e.g., via a Gateway Load Balancer endpoint) before reaching its final destination, enabling centralized inspection in a scalable and managed way. Option A (incorrect) using Amazon VPC Peering for this would lead to a complex ''n-squared'' peering mesh and would not easily facilitate centralized traffic steering through a single inspection VPC for all traffic types. Option C (incorrect) deploying AWS Network Firewall in each application VPC would decentralize the inspection, increasing operational overhead and making policy management more complex, contrary to the goal of a centralized inspection VPC. Option D (incorrect) AWS Direct Connect Gateway is used for connecting on-premises networks to multiple VPCs and does not provide the routing capabilities needed to force intra-AWS VPC traffic through a centralized inspection VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_39',
  'A company has multiple VPCs in the same AWS Region, some with overlapping IP address ranges due to historical reasons. They need to establish communication between these VPCs while resolving the IP overlap issue. Which AWS networking component is specifically designed to handle IP address translation for overlapping CIDR blocks when connecting VPCs?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC Peering with custom route tables.","AWS Transit Gateway with Network Address Translation (NAT) rules.","AWS PrivateLink for service-specific communication.","Deploying NAT Gateways in each VPC and configuring routing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Transit Gateway can handle IP overlap between VPCs by using Network Address Translation (NAT) capabilities. When VPCs with overlapping CIDR blocks are attached to a Transit Gateway, you can configure NAT to translate the IP addresses, allowing communication between them. This is a key feature for complex enterprise environments with legacy IP addressing. Option A (incorrect) VPC Peering does not support overlapping CIDR blocks; if two peered VPCs have overlapping CIDRs, communication between the overlapping ranges will fail. Option C (incorrect) AWS PrivateLink provides private connectivity to services but does not solve the general VPC-to-VPC communication problem with overlapping IP ranges. Option D (incorrect) deploying NAT Gateways in each VPC is for outbound internet access or for translating private IPs to public IPs, not for resolving overlapping private IP ranges between VPCs for direct communication through a central hub.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_40',
  'What is the primary use case for an AWS Transit Gateway?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide a managed relational database service.","To connect thousands of VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub.","To distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets.","To store and retrieve any amount of data from anywhere on the web."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the primary use case for AWS Transit Gateway, which acts as a network transit hub to interconnect your virtual private clouds (VPCs) and on-premises networks. This simplifies network management and scales connectivity. Option A describes Amazon RDS. Option C describes Elastic Load Balancing. Option D describes Amazon S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

