INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_21',
  'Which AWS service is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon Route 53","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Directory Service"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon Route 53) is the correct service. Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. It can be used to register domain names, route internet traffic to resources, and check the health of resources. Option A (Amazon S3) is an object storage service. Option C (AWS CloudFormation) is an infrastructure as code service. Option D (AWS Directory Service) provides managed Microsoft Active Directory or Simple AD in the AWS Cloud.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_22',
  'What is the primary purpose of DNSSEC in Amazon Route 53?',
  'mcq',
  '["To encrypt DNS queries for privacy","To secure DNS responses against spoofing and tampering","To improve the speed of DNS resolution","To manage private DNS records within a VPC"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To secure DNS responses against spoofing and tampering) is the primary purpose of DNSSEC in Amazon Route 53. DNSSEC adds a layer of security to DNS by using digital signatures to verify the authenticity of DNS responses, protecting against attacks like DNS cache poisoning. Option A (To encrypt DNS queries for privacy) is not the primary function of DNSSEC; it''s about integrity and authenticity. Option C (To improve the speed of DNS resolution) is a general benefit of Route 53''s global network, not specific to DNSSEC. Option D (To manage private DNS records within a VPC) is handled by Route 53 Private Hosted Zones.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_23',
  'A developer needs to manage public DNS records for their company''s domain name, example.com. Which Route 53 component should they configure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Route 53 Resolver endpoint","Private Hosted Zone","Public Hosted Zone","Traffic Policy"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Public Hosted Zone) is the correct component. A Public Hosted Zone in Amazon Route 53 is a container that holds information about how you want to route traffic for a domain and its subdomains on the internet. Option A (Route 53 Resolver endpoint) is used for hybrid DNS resolution between VPCs and on-premises networks. Option B (Private Hosted Zone) is used for managing DNS records within one or more VPCs. Option D (Traffic Policy) is used for advanced routing configurations, but it operates within a hosted zone.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_24',
  'Which AWS service allows you to create a private DNS service that resolves DNS queries for resources within your Amazon VPCs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zones","AWS Directory Service"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zones) is the correct service. Private Hosted Zones allow you to manage DNS records for your internal AWS resources, such as EC2 instances and internal load balancers, within your Amazon VPCs, without exposing those records to the public internet. Option A (Amazon CloudFront) is a CDN. Option B (AWS Global Accelerator) improves global application performance. Option D (AWS Directory Service) provides managed directories, not private DNS resolution for VPC resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_25',
  'A company has an on-premises DNS server and needs to resolve DNS queries for resources hosted in an Amazon VPC. Which Amazon Route 53 feature enables this hybrid DNS resolution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Public Hosted Zone","Alias records","Route 53 Resolver endpoints","Traffic policies"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Route 53 Resolver endpoints) is the correct feature. Route 53 Resolver endpoints allow DNS queries to be forwarded between your VPCs and your on-premises DNS servers, enabling seamless hybrid DNS resolution. Option A (Public Hosted Zone) is for public internet domains. Option B (Alias records) map one DNS record to another AWS resource. Option D (Traffic policies) are for advanced routing configurations within Route 53.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_26',
  'What is an Alias record in Amazon Route 53 primarily used for?',
  'mcq',
  '["To redirect HTTP traffic to an HTTPS endpoint","To map a domain name to an AWS resource like an ELB or S3 bucket","To perform health checks on backend instances","To secure DNS queries against spoofing"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To map a domain name to an AWS resource like an ELB or S3 bucket) is what an Alias record in Amazon Route 53 is primarily used for. Alias records are a Route 53-specific extension to DNS that allow you to map a domain name (e.g., example.com) to an AWS resource (e.g., an Elastic Load Balancer, an Amazon S3 bucket configured as a static website, or a CloudFront distribution) instead of an IP address. They behave like CNAME records but can be used for the zone apex (root domain). Option A (To redirect HTTP traffic to an HTTPS endpoint) is typically done at the application or load balancer level. Option C (To perform health checks on backend instances) is done by Route 53 health checks. Option D (To secure DNS queries against spoofing) is the purpose of DNSSEC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_27',
  'Which type of record in Amazon Route 53 allows you to point a domain or subdomain to an AWS resource, such as an Elastic Load Balancer, without incurring DNS query charges?',
  'mcq',
  '["CNAME record","A record","MX record","Alias record"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (Alias record) is the correct type. Alias records in Amazon Route 53 allow you to map your domain name to select AWS resources (like ELBs, S3 buckets configured as static websites, CloudFront distributions, etc.) and are free of charge for DNS queries. They also provide automatic updates if the underlying IP address of the AWS resource changes. Option A (CNAME record) maps a domain name to another domain name, but incurs standard DNS query charges. Option B (A record) maps a domain name to an IPv4 address. Option C (MX record) specifies mail servers for a domain.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_28',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon Route 53?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide a managed service for running containers.","To provide a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service.","To deliver content with low latency and high transfer speeds.","To manage secrets and credentials for applications."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary purpose of Amazon Route 53, which is a highly available and scalable cloud DNS web service. It can be used to register domain names, route internet traffic to resources, and check the health of resources. Option A describes Amazon ECS or Amazon EKS. Option C describes Amazon CloudFront. Option D describes AWS Secrets Manager.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_29',
  'What is an Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zone primarily used for?',
  'mcq',
  '["To route traffic to public AWS services.","To manage DNS records for resources within your Amazon VPCs.","To register new domain names on the internet.","To provide DNSSEC for public domains."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary use of an Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zone. It allows you to manage DNS records for resources within your Amazon VPCs, enabling private DNS resolution for internal applications without exposing them to the public internet. Option A is for public hosted zones. Option C is for domain registration services. Option D is a specific security feature for public domains.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_30',
  'A development team has an application running in an Amazon VPC and needs to resolve internal DNS names for services deployed within that VPC, without exposing these names to the public internet. Which Route 53 feature should they configure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Public Hosted Zone","Traffic Policies","Private Hosted Zone","Alias Records"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is a Private Hosted Zone. Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zones allow you to manage DNS records for your VPCs, providing internal DNS resolution for resources within your private network without public exposure. Option A, Public Hosted Zone, is for public-facing domains. Option B, Traffic Policies, define routing rules for complex routing scenarios. Option D, Alias Records, map DNS names to AWS resources like ELBs or S3 buckets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_31',
  'A company needs to manage DNS records for both their public-facing website and their internal applications within AWS VPCs. They require a highly available and scalable DNS solution that supports various routing policies, health checks, and secure DNS extensions. Which AWS service should a developer choose to meet these public and private DNS requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 DNS","Amazon Route 53","AWS Cloud Map","AWS App Mesh"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. It allows you to manage public DNS for your domains using public hosted zones and private DNS for your internal applications within VPCs using private hosted zones. It supports various routing policies (e.g., simple, weighted, latency-based, geolocation), health checks, and DNSSEC for secure DNS. Option A (incorrect) Amazon EC2 instances use DNS provided by the VPC, but there isn''t a dedicated ''Amazon EC2 DNS'' service for managing custom public or private domains. Option C (incorrect) AWS Cloud Map is a service discovery service for cloud resources, allowing applications to find and connect to services, but it''s not a full-fledged public/private DNS management service like Route 53. Option D (incorrect) AWS App Mesh is a service mesh that provides application-level networking to make it easy to run microservices, but it does not provide DNS resolution for public or private domains.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_32',
  'A company is migrating its on-premises applications to AWS and needs to ensure seamless DNS resolution for both cloud and on-premises resources. They require a solution that allows their on-premises DNS servers to resolve private DNS names hosted in AWS Route 53 private hosted zones, and vice versa. Which AWS Route 53 feature is essential for this hybrid DNS resolution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Route 53 public hosted zones.","Route 53 alias records.","Route 53 Resolver endpoints.","Route 53 traffic policies."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) Route 53 Resolver endpoints are crucial for hybrid DNS resolution. They allow you to forward DNS queries between your VPCs and your on-premises DNS servers. An inbound endpoint allows on-premises DNS servers to query Route 53 for DNS records in private hosted zones, and an outbound endpoint allows VPCs to forward DNS queries to on-premises DNS servers. Option A (incorrect) Route 53 public hosted zones are for public-facing domains and do not facilitate private DNS resolution between on-premises and VPCs. Option B (incorrect) Route 53 alias records map a domain name to an AWS resource (like an ELB or S3 bucket) and are a type of record, not a mechanism for hybrid DNS resolution. Option D (incorrect) Route 53 traffic policies allow you to create complex routing configurations for DNS queries, but they rely on the underlying DNS resolution mechanism provided by Resolver endpoints for hybrid scenarios.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_33',
  'A developer needs to configure a highly available DNS solution for a public-facing web application. The solution must automatically fail over to a healthy endpoint if the primary endpoint becomes unhealthy. Which Route 53 feature should be used to monitor the health of the application endpoints and redirect traffic accordingly?',
  'mcq',
  '["Simple routing policy.","Weighted routing policy.","Health checks with failover routing policy.","Geolocation routing policy."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) Route 53 health checks can monitor the health of your application endpoints. When combined with a failover routing policy, Route 53 automatically redirects traffic from an unhealthy primary endpoint to a healthy secondary endpoint, ensuring high availability for your application. Option A (incorrect) Simple routing policy is for single resources and does not support health checks or failover. Option B (incorrect) Weighted routing policy distributes traffic among multiple resources based on assigned weights, but it does not inherently perform health checks or automatic failover without additional configuration. Option D (incorrect) Geolocation routing policy routes traffic based on the geographic location of your users, but it doesn''t automatically fail over based on endpoint health without being combined with health checks and a failover mechanism.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_34',
  'A company wants to enhance the security of their public DNS records managed by Amazon Route 53 by protecting against DNS spoofing and cache poisoning attacks. Which Route 53 feature should they enable to cryptographically sign their DNS records and ensure their authenticity?',
  'mcq',
  '["Alias records.","Health checks.","DNSSEC (DNS Security Extensions).","Traffic policies."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) DNSSEC (DNS Security Extensions) is a suite of IETF specifications that add a layer of security to the DNS protocol by cryptographically signing DNS records. Enabling DNSSEC in Route 53 helps protect against DNS spoofing and cache poisoning attacks by ensuring that DNS responses are authentic and have not been tampered with. Option A (incorrect) Alias records map a domain name to an AWS resource, which is a routing feature, not a security feature for DNS record authenticity. Option B (incorrect) Health checks monitor the health of endpoints and are used for routing decisions, not for securing DNS records themselves. Option D (incorrect) Traffic policies allow for complex routing configurations, but they do not provide cryptographic security for DNS records.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_35',
  'A developer is setting up a new application in a multi-account AWS environment. The application needs to resolve private DNS names for services hosted in a shared services VPC in a different AWS account. The solution must be centralized and avoid creating individual DNS records in each application VPC. Which Route 53 feature, combined with AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM), should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["Public hosted zones.","Private hosted zones shared via RAM.","Alias records pointing to shared service endpoints.","Outbound Resolver endpoints in each application VPC."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Route 53 private hosted zones can be shared across multiple AWS accounts within an AWS Organization using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM). This allows applications in different VPCs and accounts to resolve private DNS names defined in the shared private hosted zone, centralizing DNS management and avoiding duplication. Option A (incorrect) Public hosted zones are for public-facing domains and are not suitable for resolving private DNS names within VPCs. Option C (incorrect) Alias records point to specific AWS resources. While useful, they don''t provide a centralized mechanism for sharing an entire private DNS namespace across accounts. Option D (incorrect) Outbound Resolver endpoints are used to forward DNS queries from VPCs to on-premises DNS servers. While a component of hybrid DNS, they don''t directly enable sharing private hosted zones across accounts for intra-AWS resolution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_36',
  'A developer is configuring a public DNS zone in Amazon Route 53 for a new website. They need to ensure that the domain name always points to an Application Load Balancer (ALB) that distributes traffic to their web servers, even if the ALB''s underlying IP addresses change. Which type of Route 53 record should be used to achieve this without incurring additional DNS query charges?',
  'mcq',
  '["A record.","CNAME record.","Alias record.","MX record."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) An Alias record in Amazon Route 53 is a Route 53-specific extension to DNS. It can point to AWS resources like an Application Load Balancer (ALB), Amazon S3 buckets, or CloudFront distributions. Alias records automatically track the underlying IP addresses of the target resource, ensuring that the domain always points to the correct endpoint even if the IP addresses change. Crucially, Route 53 does not charge for alias queries to AWS resources. Option A (incorrect) An A record maps a domain name to a fixed IPv4 address. If the ALB''s IP addresses change, the A record would need manual updates. Option B (incorrect) A CNAME record maps one domain name to another. While it can point to an ALB''s DNS name, it cannot be used for the zone apex (e.g., example.com) and incurs standard DNS query charges. Option D (incorrect) An MX record specifies mail servers for a domain and is unrelated to pointing a domain to a web application load balancer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_37',
  'What is the primary capability of Amazon Route 53?',
  'mcq',
  '["To manage and monitor network traffic between VPCs.","To provide a highly available and scalable Domain Name System (DNS) web service.","To encrypt data at rest and in transit for various AWS services.","To automate the deployment and scaling of applications."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the primary capability of Amazon Route 53, which is a highly available and scalable cloud DNS web service. It translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses. Option A describes services like AWS Transit Gateway or VPC Flow Logs. Option C describes AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS). Option D describes services like AWS Elastic Beanstalk or AWS Auto Scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_2_38',
  'A developer needs to manage DNS records for both public-facing web applications and internal resources within a VPC, ensuring secure and reliable name resolution. Which AWS service is best suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 instances running BIND DNS server","AWS Directory Service","Amazon Route 53","AWS PrivateLink"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon Route 53, which allows you to manage DNS for both public domains (using public hosted zones) and private domains within your VPCs (using private hosted zones). It is a highly available and scalable managed service. Option A, running BIND on EC2, would introduce significant operational overhead and management complexity. Option B, AWS Directory Service, provides managed Microsoft Active Directory or Simple AD, which includes DNS but is primarily for identity management, not general-purpose public/private DNS management. Option D, AWS PrivateLink, enables private connectivity between VPCs and services, but it is not a DNS management service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_50_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_20',
  'Which type of Elastic Load Balancer is best suited for load balancing HTTP and HTTPS traffic and provides advanced routing features based on request content?',
  'mcq',
  '["Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Application Load Balancer (ALB)) is best suited for load balancing HTTP and HTTPS traffic. ALBs operate at the application layer (Layer 7 of the OSI model) and provide advanced routing features, such as path-based routing, host-based routing, and URL-based routing. Option A (Network Load Balancer (NLB)) operates at the transport layer (Layer 4) and is optimized for extreme performance and static IP addresses. Option C (Classic Load Balancer (CLB)) is a previous generation load balancer, still available but generally superseded by ALB and NLB. Option D (Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)) is used for deploying, scaling, and managing third-party virtual appliances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_21',
  'A developer needs to distribute incoming traffic across a group of Amazon EC2 instances, ensuring high performance and low latency for TCP and UDP protocols. Which type of Elastic Load Balancer should they choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Network Load Balancer (NLB)) is the correct choice. NLBs operate at the transport layer (Layer 4) and are designed for extreme performance and low latency, making them ideal for TCP and UDP traffic where high throughput is critical. Option A (Application Load Balancer (ALB)) operates at Layer 7 and is best for HTTP/HTTPS. Option B (Classic Load Balancer (CLB)) is an older generation and less performant for this use case. Option D (Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)) is specifically for deploying and managing virtual appliances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_22',
  'What is a ''target group'' in the context of Elastic Load Balancing?',
  'mcq',
  '["A collection of DNS records for a load balancer","A logical grouping of backend instances or IP addresses that receive traffic from a load balancer","A security group that controls access to the load balancer","A set of rules for routing traffic to different load balancers"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (A logical grouping of backend instances or IP addresses that receive traffic from a load balancer) is the correct definition of a target group. In Elastic Load Balancing, a target group is used to route requests to one or more registered targets, such as EC2 instances, IP addresses, or AWS Lambda functions. Each listener rule specifies a target group, and traffic is forwarded to the targets within that group. Option A (A collection of DNS records for a load balancer) is incorrect. Option C (A security group that controls access to the load balancer) is a separate security construct. Option D (A set of rules for routing traffic to different load balancers) is incorrect; rules route to target groups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  100,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_23',
  'A web application needs to distribute incoming HTTP requests to multiple Amazon EC2 instances across different Availability Zones for high availability. Which AWS service is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon RDS","AWS Lambda","Elastic Load Balancing","Amazon SQS"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Elastic Load Balancing) is the correct service. ELB automatically distributes incoming application traffic across multiple targets, such as Amazon EC2 instances, in one or more Availability Zones, ensuring high availability and fault tolerance. Option A (Amazon RDS) is a relational database service. Option B (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service. Option D (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  101,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_24',
  'A developer wants to ensure that a user''s session is consistently routed to the same backend EC2 instance behind an Application Load Balancer. Which feature should they configure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Cross-zone load balancing","Health checks","Sticky sessions (session affinity)","Target group weighting"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Sticky sessions (session affinity)) is the correct feature. Sticky sessions ensure that requests from a specific client are consistently routed to the same target (e.g., EC2 instance) behind an Application Load Balancer. This is crucial for applications that store session-specific data locally on the server. Option A (Cross-zone load balancing) distributes traffic evenly across targets regardless of AZ. Option B (Health checks) monitor the health of targets and remove unhealthy ones from rotation. Option D (Target group weighting) is used for routing a specified percentage of traffic to different target groups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  102,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_25',
  'A developer is building a microservices-based application running on Amazon EC2 instances and needs to distribute incoming HTTP/S traffic to different services based on the URL path. Which type of Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) should be used to meet this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is an Application Load Balancer (ALB). ALBs operate at Layer 7 (application layer) and can route traffic based on content-based rules, such as URL paths or host headers, making them ideal for microservices architectures. Option A, Network Load Balancer (NLB), operates at Layer 4 (transport layer) and routes based on IP protocol data. Option C, Classic Load Balancer (CLB), is a legacy load balancer with limited Layer 7 features. Option D, Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB), is used for deploying, managing, and scaling third-party virtual network appliances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  103,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_26',
  'A company requires a load balancer for a high-performance, low-latency application that primarily handles TCP traffic. The application needs to process millions of requests per second. Which type of Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) is best suited for this workload?',
  'mcq',
  '["Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is a Network Load Balancer (NLB). NLBs are designed for extreme performance, handling millions of requests per second with ultra-low latency for TCP, UDP, and TLS traffic. This makes them ideal for high-throughput, low-latency workloads. Option A, Application Load Balancer (ALB), is for HTTP/S traffic and Layer 7 routing. Option C, Classic Load Balancer (CLB), is a legacy option. Option D, Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB), is for virtual network appliances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  104,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_27',
  'What is the primary use case for a Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)?',
  'mcq',
  '["To distribute HTTP/S traffic to web servers.","To provide a highly available entry point for containerized applications.","To deploy, manage, and scale third-party virtual network appliances.","To balance traffic across multiple AWS Regions."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes the primary use case for a Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB). GWLB is specifically designed to simplify the deployment, scaling, and management of third-party virtual network appliances such as firewalls, intrusion detection systems, and deep packet inspection systems. Option A describes an Application Load Balancer (ALB). Option B is a general load balancer function, often handled by ALB or NLB. Option D is typically handled by services like AWS Global Accelerator or Route 53.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  105,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_28',
  'A security team needs to insert a third-party firewall appliance into the network path to inspect all incoming and outgoing traffic for a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances. They require a scalable and highly available solution that can transparently route traffic through this appliance. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)","AWS WAF"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is a Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB). GWLB is specifically designed for deploying and scaling third-party virtual network appliances, like firewalls, by transparently routing all traffic through them. It uses a GENEVE encapsulation protocol. Option A, Application Load Balancer (ALB), is for HTTP/S traffic. Option B, Network Load Balancer (NLB), is for high-performance TCP/UDP traffic but doesn''t have the appliance insertion capability of GWLB. Option D, AWS WAF, is a web application firewall that protects against common web exploits, but it''s not a generic network appliance insertion point.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
