INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_29',
  'A company needs to distribute incoming HTTP and HTTPS traffic across multiple Amazon EC2 instances running a web application. The solution must support path-based routing, host-based routing, and SSL termination. Which AWS load balancing service is best suited for these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Application Load Balancer (ALB) operates at the application layer (Layer 7) of the OSI model. It supports advanced routing features such as path-based routing (e.g., /users to one target group, /products to another) and host-based routing (e.g., api.example.com to one target group, www.example.com to another). It also natively supports SSL/TLS termination, making it ideal for HTTP/HTTPS web applications. Option A (incorrect) Network Load Balancer (NLB) operates at the transport layer (Layer 4) and is optimized for extreme performance and static IP addresses. It does not support application-layer routing features like path-based or host-based routing. Option C (incorrect) Classic Load Balancer (CLB) is a previous generation load balancer that supports both Layer 4 and Layer 7, but it lacks the advanced routing capabilities of ALB and is generally not recommended for new deployments. Option D (incorrect) Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) operates at Layer 3 (network layer) and Layer 4 (transport layer) and is specifically designed to deploy, scale, and manage virtual appliances such as firewalls, intrusion detection systems, and deep packet inspection systems, not for distributing HTTP/HTTPS traffic to web applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  107,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_30',
  'A developer is designing a highly available and scalable architecture for a new microservices application. Each microservice runs in separate Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups. The application requires sticky sessions for certain user interactions and needs to integrate with AWS WAF for security. Which AWS load balancing service should be chosen to meet these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Application Load Balancer (ALB) operates at Layer 7 and is ideal for microservices architectures. It supports sticky sessions (session affinity) at the application level, allowing requests from a user to be routed to the same target. ALB also integrates seamlessly with AWS WAF for web application security. It can distribute traffic to multiple target groups, each potentially representing a different microservice, and supports Auto Scaling groups. Option A (incorrect) Network Load Balancer (NLB) operates at Layer 4 and is optimized for extreme performance and static IP addresses. It does not support sticky sessions at the application layer or direct integration with AWS WAF. Option C (incorrect) Classic Load Balancer (CLB) is a legacy load balancer that supports sticky sessions and WAF integration, but ALB offers more advanced features, better performance, and is the recommended choice for new Layer 7 applications. Option D (incorrect) Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) is designed for deploying and managing virtual network appliances, not for distributing application traffic to microservices with sticky sessions or WAF integration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  108,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_31',
  'A company needs to distribute millions of requests per second to a fleet of backend servers for a real-time gaming application. The application requires extremely low latency and needs to preserve the client''s source IP address. Which AWS load balancing service is optimized for this high-performance, Layer 4 traffic distribution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) Network Load Balancer (NLB) operates at Layer 4 (transport layer) and is optimized for extreme performance, handling millions of requests per second with ultra-low latency. It preserves the client''s source IP address, which is crucial for many applications, including real-time gaming, that rely on client IP for authentication or logging. Option A (incorrect) Application Load Balancer (ALB) operates at Layer 7 and is designed for HTTP/HTTPS traffic with advanced routing features, but it adds more latency and does not preserve the client''s source IP address by default (it uses X-Forwarded-For headers). Option B (incorrect) Classic Load Balancer (CLB) is a legacy load balancer that does not offer the same performance or features as NLB for high-throughput Layer 4 traffic. Option D (incorrect) Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) is designed for deploying and managing virtual network appliances, not for direct high-performance distribution of application traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  109,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_32',
  'What is the primary purpose of an Application Load Balancer (ALB)?',
  'mcq',
  '["To distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets at the application layer (Layer 7).","To provide a managed relational database service with high availability.","To manage and distribute network traffic at the connection layer (Layer 4).","To store and retrieve large amounts of unstructured data."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes the primary purpose of an Application Load Balancer (ALB), which operates at the application layer (Layer 7) and allows for advanced routing based on HTTP/HTTPS headers, paths, and hostnames. Option B describes Amazon RDS. Option C describes a Network Load Balancer (NLB), which operates at Layer 4. Option D describes Amazon S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  110,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_3_33',
  'A web application needs to route requests to different backend services (e.g., EC2 instances, AWS Lambda functions) based on the URL path or host header. Which type of Elastic Load Balancer should be used to fulfill this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is an Application Load Balancer (ALB). ALBs operate at Layer 7 (the application layer) and support content-based routing rules, allowing traffic to be directed to different target groups based on URL paths, host headers, or other HTTP/HTTPS attributes. This is ideal for microservices or container-based applications. Option A, Classic Load Balancer (CLB), is an older generation load balancer with limited Layer 7 routing capabilities. Option B, Network Load Balancer (NLB), operates at Layer 4 (the transport layer) and routes traffic based on IP protocol data, not URL paths. Option D, Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB), is designed for deploying and managing third-party virtual appliances, not for application-level routing to diverse backend services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  111,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

