INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_39',
  'Which AWS service is primarily used to automate the provisioning of AWS resources through code, supporting repeatable deployments of network infrastructure?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Systems Manager","AWS Config","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudFormation) is the correct service for automating the provisioning of AWS resources through code, allowing for repeatable and efficient deployments of infrastructure, including network components like VPCs and Transit Gateways. Option B (AWS Systems Manager) is used for operational tasks like patching, automation, and configuration management, not primary infrastructure provisioning. Option C (AWS Config) is for assessing, auditing, and evaluating the configurations of AWS resources. Option D (AWS OpsWorks) is a configuration management service that uses Chef and Puppet to automate server configuration, deployment, and management, which is different from infrastructure provisioning via code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_40',
  'A development team needs to deploy a new VPC with specific subnets, route tables, and security groups consistently across multiple environments. Which AWS tool provides an infrastructure-as-code approach for this requirement with the LEAST manual effort?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK)","AWS Management Console","AWS Command Line Interface (CLI)","AWS SDKs"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK)) allows developers to define cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages, which then synthesizes into AWS CloudFormation templates. This provides an infrastructure-as-code approach with the least manual effort for consistent deployments. Option B (AWS Management Console) involves manual clicking and is not an infrastructure-as-code approach, leading to inconsistencies and higher manual effort. Option C (AWS Command Line Interface (CLI)) allows scripting, but directly managing resources with individual CLI commands for complex infrastructure is more error-prone and requires more effort to maintain than CDK or CloudFormation. Option D (AWS SDKs) enable programmatic interaction with AWS services, but defining infrastructure directly with SDK calls is more verbose and less abstract than using CDK.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_41',
  'Which feature of AWS CloudFormation allows developers to avoid hardcoding values and reuse templates for different environments by providing parameters at deployment time?',
  'mcq',
  '["Parameters","Outputs","Conditions","Mappings"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Parameters) in AWS CloudFormation allows you to input custom values to your template each time you create or update a stack, preventing hardcoding and enabling template reuse across environments. Option B (Outputs) are values that you can export from your stack to be referenced by other stacks or to view after deployment. Option C (Conditions) allow you to define whether resources are created or properties are applied based on specific criteria. Option D (Mappings) are used to define a set of keys and associated values that you can use to specify conditional parameter values, but they don''t directly provide input values at deployment like Parameters.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_42',
  'A developer wants to integrate network infrastructure changes into a CI/CD pipeline to ensure automated testing and deployment. Which AWS service is best suited for managing the source code for CloudFormation templates in this pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeCommit","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeBuild"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS CodeCommit) is a fully managed source control service that hosts secure Git-based repositories, making it ideal for storing CloudFormation templates and integrating with CI/CD pipelines. Option B (AWS CodeDeploy) automates code deployments to various compute services like Amazon EC2, AWS Fargate, and AWS Lambda, but it''s not for source code management. Option C (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates the entire release process, including source, build, test, and deploy stages, but it relies on a source control service like CodeCommit. Option D (AWS CodeBuild) compiles source code, runs tests, and produces deployable artifacts, but it also relies on a source control service for input.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_43',
  'To ensure repeatability and efficiency when deploying network infrastructure, a development team should prioritize which practice?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using infrastructure as code (IaC) tools like AWS CloudFormation.","Manually configuring resources through the AWS Management Console.","Writing detailed step-by-step documentation for manual deployment.","Employing shell scripts for individual resource creation."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Using infrastructure as code (IaC) tools like AWS CloudFormation) is the best practice for ensuring repeatability and efficiency in network infrastructure deployments. IaC defines infrastructure in code, allowing for version control, automated testing, and consistent deployments. Option B (Manually configuring resources through the AWS Management Console) is prone to human error and lacks repeatability. Option C (Writing detailed step-by-step documentation for manual deployment) is better than no documentation but still relies on manual execution, which is error-prone and inefficient. Option D (Employing shell scripts for individual resource creation) can be a form of automation but often lacks the declarative nature, state management, and rollback capabilities of dedicated IaC tools like CloudFormation, making it less robust for complex network infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_44',
  'A company needs to provision a new VPC and associated networking components in a secure and automated manner. Which AWS service allows defining this infrastructure in a declarative template, ensuring all resources are created consistently?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","Amazon EC2","AWS Lambda","Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudFormation) is the service specifically designed for defining and provisioning infrastructure as code in a declarative template, ensuring consistent and automated creation of resources like VPCs and networking components. Option B (Amazon EC2) is for launching virtual servers, not for defining and provisioning entire network infrastructure. Option C (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service for running code, not for infrastructure provisioning. Option D (Amazon S3) is an object storage service, unrelated to network infrastructure provisioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_45',
  'Which AWS service allows you to define your cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages like Python or TypeScript, which then synthesizes into AWS CloudFormation templates?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK)","AWS AppSync","AWS Amplify","AWS Step Functions"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK)) is the correct service. It is an open-source software development framework to define your cloud application resources using familiar programming languages. It then provisions these resources using AWS CloudFormation. Option B (AWS AppSync) is a fully managed service that makes it easy to create flexible APIs to securely access, manipulate, and combine data from one or more data sources. Option C (AWS Amplify) is a set of tools and services that enables mobile and front-end web developers to build secure, scalable full-stack applications. Option D (AWS Step Functions) is a serverless workflow service that lets you combine AWS Lambda functions and other AWS services to build business-critical applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_46',
  'A developer is creating a CloudFormation template for a VPC and wants to dynamically choose different CIDR blocks based on the deployment environment (e.g., ''dev'' or ''prod''). Which CloudFormation feature would best facilitate this dynamic selection?',
  'mcq',
  '["Mappings","Conditions","Outputs","Resources"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Mappings) is the best CloudFormation feature for this scenario. Mappings allow you to define a set of keys and associated values that you can use to specify conditional parameter values. You could define a map with environment names as top-level keys and CIDR blocks as values, then reference this map based on an environment parameter. Option B (Conditions) are used to conditionally create resources or apply properties, not to select values from a predefined set based on an input. Option C (Outputs) are values exported from the stack, not for dynamic input selection during creation. Option D (Resources) is where you declare the AWS resources to be created, not a mechanism for dynamic value selection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_47',
  'An enterprise needs to rapidly provision new VPCs for development teams, each requiring connectivity to a central services VPC via an existing AWS Transit Gateway. The process must be fully automated, repeatable, and minimize manual configuration errors. The solution should ensure that routes to the new VPCs are automatically propagated to the Transit Gateway route table, and the entire setup should be defined as code. Which approach offers the LEAST operational overhead for network provisioning while meeting these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually create VPCs, Transit Gateway attachments, and route table entries for each new development team using the AWS Management Console.","Develop AWS CloudFormation templates to define the VPCs, Transit Gateway attachments, and use Transit Gateway route table propagation to automatically update routes.","Write custom scripts using the AWS CLI to create VPCs and configure Transit Gateway attachments, then manually update Transit Gateway route tables.","Utilize AWS Systems Manager Automation documents to orchestrate the creation of VPCs and Transit Gateway attachments, requiring manual route table updates."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most effective solution. AWS CloudFormation allows defining VPCs and Transit Gateway attachments as Infrastructure as Code. Crucially, Transit Gateway route table propagation automatically updates the Transit Gateway''s route tables with routes to attached VPCs, significantly reducing manual configuration and operational overhead for dynamic environments. Option A is incorrect because manual configuration via the AWS Management Console is prone to errors, not repeatable, and has high operational overhead. Option C is incorrect because while AWS CLI scripts offer automation, they typically require explicit commands for route table updates, which adds complexity compared to automatic propagation. Option D is incorrect because AWS Systems Manager Automation documents can orchestrate tasks, but without explicit integration for automatic route propagation, manual route table updates would still be necessary, increasing overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_48',
  'A large organization is adopting Infrastructure as Code (IaC) for its network infrastructure across multiple AWS accounts and regions. They need a programmatic way to define VPCs, subnets, route tables, and associated security components, ensuring consistency and version control. The solution should allow for complex logic, use a familiar programming language, and integrate seamlessly with existing development workflows for MAXIMIZING development velocity and consistency across environments. Which AWS tool is best suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation with YAML templates.","AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM) templates.","AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK) using TypeScript or Python.","Terraform with HCL configurations."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most suitable choice. The AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK) allows developers to define cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages like TypeScript or Python. It synthesizes these definitions into CloudFormation templates, offering strong typing, object-oriented constructs, and integration with existing development tools, which maximizes development velocity and consistency. Option A is incorrect because while AWS CloudFormation with YAML templates is a valid IaC tool, it uses a declarative language that can be less flexible for complex logic compared to a full programming language. Option B is incorrect because AWS SAM is specifically for serverless applications and not a general-purpose tool for defining entire network infrastructures. Option D is incorrect because while Terraform is a powerful IaC tool, it is not an AWS-native service and might introduce additional management overhead for an organization primarily focused on AWS-native solutions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_49',
  'A company has established multiple AWS Direct Connect connections to a Direct Connect Gateway in a central AWS Region. They now need to extend this on-premises connectivity to several Transit Gateways across different AWS Regions, each serving multiple VPCs. The goal is to automate the association of the Direct Connect Gateway with these Transit Gateways, ensuring that the entire setup is repeatable and defined as code. What is the MOST efficient way to automate this complex cross-region and cross-service association?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually create Direct Connect Gateway associations with each Transit Gateway using the AWS Management Console.","Use AWS CloudFormation templates to define the Direct Connect Gateway associations with the Transit Gateways in each respective region.","Write custom AWS CLI scripts to create the Direct Connect Gateway associations and manage them in a version control system.","Implement AWS Systems Manager Run Command to execute API calls for creating the associations on a scheduled basis."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and scalable approach. AWS CloudFormation allows defining the association between a Direct Connect Gateway and a Transit Gateway as infrastructure code. This ensures repeatability, reduces manual errors, and supports deployments across multiple regions consistently. Option A is incorrect because manual configuration is not scalable, prone to errors, and lacks version control. Option C is incorrect because while AWS CLI scripts can automate the process, CloudFormation provides a higher-level, declarative approach that manages the lifecycle of the resources, including updates and deletions, more robustly. Option D is incorrect because AWS Systems Manager Run Command is for executing commands on instances, not for defining and managing infrastructure resources like Direct Connect Gateway associations as code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_50',
  'A development team is creating AWS CloudFormation templates to deploy a standardized three-tier application architecture, including VPCs, subnets, and security groups. They need to ensure that network CIDR blocks, subnet IDs, and security group IDs are dynamically generated or referenced, rather than hardcoded, to support deployments into different environments (dev, test, prod) without template modification. The primary goal is to MAXIMIZE template reusability and flexibility. Which CloudFormation feature or AWS service integration is most appropriate for achieving this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Hardcode all network values directly into the CloudFormation template and create separate templates for each environment.","Use CloudFormation Parameters to accept network CIDR blocks and other identifiers as input during stack creation.","Store network configurations in AWS Secrets Manager and retrieve them using a custom resource during template deployment.","Embed AWS Lambda functions within the CloudFormation template to generate random CIDR blocks and assign them to subnets."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution. CloudFormation Parameters allow you to input values, such as network CIDR blocks, during stack creation or update. This makes templates highly reusable across different environments without modification, as the specific network configurations can be provided at deployment time, maximizing flexibility. Option A is incorrect because hardcoding values and creating separate templates for each environment defeats the purpose of reusability and increases maintenance overhead. Option C is incorrect because AWS Secrets Manager is primarily for sensitive data like credentials, not typically for non-sensitive network configuration parameters, and using a custom resource for this adds unnecessary complexity. Option D is incorrect because dynamically generating random CIDR blocks with Lambda functions is generally not a best practice for network design, as it can lead to IP address conflicts and makes network planning difficult.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_51',
  'An organization is implementing a GitOps model for its network infrastructure. Network configurations (VPCs, Transit Gateway attachments, Route 53 records) are stored in a Git repository. Any pull request merged into the main branch should automatically trigger a deployment pipeline to update the AWS network infrastructure, ensuring changes are reviewed and applied consistently. The objective is to ensure reliable, auditable, and automated network deployments. Which AWS service combination best facilitates this CI/CD workflow?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeCommit for source control, AWS CodeBuild for template validation, and AWS CloudFormation for deployment.","Amazon S3 for storing templates, AWS Lambda for deployment, and Amazon EventBridge for triggering.","AWS CodeArtifact for package management, AWS CodeDeploy for application deployment, and AWS Step Functions for orchestration.","AWS Systems Manager Change Manager for approvals, AWS Config for compliance, and AWS OpsWorks for configuration management."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the most suitable CI/CD workflow for Infrastructure as Code. AWS CodeCommit serves as the Git repository for network configurations. AWS CodeBuild can be used to validate CloudFormation templates (e.g., cfn-lint) or synthesize AWS CDK applications. Finally, AWS CloudFormation is the core service for deploying and managing the network infrastructure defined in the templates. This combination ensures version control, automated testing, and reliable, auditable deployments. Option B is incorrect because while these services can be used for deployment, they don''t form a cohesive CI/CD pipeline for IaC directly from a Git repository. Option C is incorrect because CodeArtifact, CodeDeploy, and Step Functions are primarily for application deployment and orchestration, not directly for IaC pipeline management. Option D is incorrect because these services are focused on change management, compliance, and configuration management, rather than the core CI/CD pipeline for infrastructure deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_52',
  'A security and compliance team requires that all VPC Flow Logs from newly provisioned VPCs are automatically configured to publish to a central Amazon S3 bucket in a dedicated logging account. This must be done programmatically and consistently across all new VPC deployments to ensure comprehensive network traffic monitoring with MINIMAL manual intervention. Which automated solution effectively addresses this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually configure VPC Flow Logs for each new VPC through the AWS Management Console, specifying the central S3 bucket.","Include VPC Flow Log configuration as a resource within AWS CloudFormation templates used to provision new VPCs, targeting the central S3 bucket.","Develop an AWS Lambda function triggered by Amazon EventBridge when a new VPC is created, which then configures Flow Logs.","Use AWS Systems Manager State Manager to periodically check for new VPCs and apply Flow Log configurations."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most straightforward and integrated automated solution. By including the VPC Flow Log resource directly within the AWS CloudFormation template that provisions the VPC, the Flow Logs are automatically configured to publish to the specified central S3 bucket as part of the VPC creation process. This ensures consistency and minimizes manual intervention. Option A is incorrect because manual configuration is not scalable, error-prone, and does not meet the automation requirement. Option C is a viable event-driven approach but adds an extra layer of complexity (Lambda function, EventBridge rule) compared to declarative definition within CloudFormation. Option D is incorrect because AWS Systems Manager State Manager is for configuration management of instances, not for declarative provisioning of network resources like VPC Flow Logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_53',
  'A development team needs to automate the provisioning and management of their network infrastructure, including VPCs, subnets, and route tables, to ensure consistency across multiple environments. Which AWS service is primarily designed for defining and deploying this infrastructure as code?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Systems Manager","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudFormation) is the correct choice because it allows developers to define their AWS infrastructure as code using templates, enabling automated and consistent provisioning of resources like VPCs, subnets, and route tables across various environments. Option B (AWS Systems Manager) is used for operational tasks like patching, automation, and configuration management of EC2 instances and on-premises servers, not for defining network infrastructure as code. Option C (AWS CodeDeploy) automates code deployments to various compute services, which is distinct from infrastructure provisioning. Option D (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of AWS resources, helping with compliance and governance, but it does not provision infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_4_54',
  'A software company is building a new application that requires a consistent and version-controlled network setup, including a VPC, subnets, and security groups, to be deployed across development, staging, and production environments. The team wants to minimize manual configuration errors and ensure repeatability. Which AWS service is the MOST appropriate for achieving this goal with infrastructure as code?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CLI with custom scripts","AWS CloudFormation","Amazon EC2 Image Builder","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS CloudFormation) is the most appropriate service. It allows developers to define their entire AWS infrastructure, including networking components like VPCs, subnets, and security groups, in declarative templates. This enables automated, consistent, and repeatable deployments across environments, supports version control for infrastructure definitions, and minimizes manual errors. Option A (AWS CLI with custom scripts) can automate tasks but lacks the declarative nature, state management, and built-in rollback capabilities of CloudFormation, making it less robust for complex infrastructure as code. Option C (Amazon EC2 Image Builder) is used for automating the creation, management, and deployment of customized, secure, and up-to-date server images, not for provisioning network infrastructure. Option D (AWS OpsWorks) is a configuration management service that uses Chef or Puppet to automate server configuration, deployment, and management, which is not its primary use case for defining network infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

