  'm_51_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_33',
  'What capability does AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) provide for Amazon Route 53 private hosted zones?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows sharing private hosted zones with other AWS accounts or within an AWS Organization.","It automatically creates public DNS records for all resources in a VPC.","It enables DNSSEC signing for private hosted zones to enhance security.","It provides a centralized dashboard for monitoring all DNS queries across accounts."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes the capability of AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) for Route 53 private hosted zones. RAM allows you to share your private hosted zones with other AWS accounts or within your AWS Organization, facilitating centralized DNS management for multi-account architectures. Option B is incorrect; private hosted zones are not public. Option C is a separate feature of Route 53. Option D is a monitoring aspect, not a core RAM capability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_34',
  'A company needs to resolve DNS queries for its on-premises network to AWS resources within a VPC. Which Amazon Route 53 feature should be configured to enable this private resolution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint.","Route 53 public hosted zone.","Route 53 latency routing policy.","Route 53 health checks."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is a Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint. This allows DNS queries from your on-premises network to be forwarded to Route 53 Resolver for resolution of private DNS names in your VPCs. Option B, a public hosted zone, is for public internet resolution. Option C, latency routing, is a traffic management policy. Option D, health checks, monitor resource health.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_35',
  'What is the primary function of Amazon Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["To forward DNS queries from a VPC to an on-premises DNS server.","To allow on-premises DNS servers to forward queries to Route 53 for resolution within a VPC.","To provide public DNS resolution for internet-facing applications.","To configure conditional forwarding rules for specific domain names."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary function of Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoints. They enable DNS queries originating from on-premises networks to be forwarded to Route 53 Resolver for resolution of DNS records within your Amazon VPCs. Option A describes outbound endpoints. Option C is handled by public hosted zones. Option D is a separate Resolver rule configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_36',
  'Which feature of Amazon Route 53 enhances the security of DNS responses by preventing DNS spoofing and cache poisoning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Weighted routing.","Private hosted zones.","DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC).","Traffic flow."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC). DNSSEC adds a layer of security to DNS by digitally signing DNS responses, which helps to prevent DNS spoofing and cache poisoning attacks. Option A is a routing policy. Option B provides private resolution but doesn''t inherently prevent spoofing for the zone itself. Option D is a traffic management feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_37',
  'A developer needs to ensure that internal DNS names for resources in a shared services VPC are resolvable from application VPCs in different accounts. Which AWS service combination would achieve this with minimal configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53 public hosted zone and Amazon EC2 instances.","Amazon Route 53 private hosted zone shared via AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).","AWS Direct Connect and on-premises DNS servers.","Amazon CloudFront and AWS WAF."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution. An Amazon Route 53 private hosted zone can be associated with the shared services VPC, and then shared with other AWS accounts using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM). This allows resources in the application VPCs to resolve DNS names in the shared services VPC privately. Option A uses a public zone, which is not suitable for internal resolution. Option C is for on-premises to AWS connectivity, not inter-VPC. Option D is for content delivery and web application firewall.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_38',
  'Which AWS service allows you to manage DNS records for your domains and configure health checks for routing traffic to healthy endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon VPC"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon Route 53) is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. It is used to manage DNS records for domains and provides health checking capabilities to route traffic to healthy endpoints. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated network connection from your premises to AWS. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) connects VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub. Option D (Amazon VPC) allows you to provision a logically isolated section of the AWS Cloud where you can launch AWS resources in a virtual network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_39',
  'A company operates applications in multiple AWS Regions and wants to direct users to the Region closest to them for optimal performance. Which Amazon Route 53 routing policy should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["Latency-based routing","Weighted routing","Geolocation routing","Failover routing"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Latency-based routing) is designed to route user requests to the AWS Region that provides the lowest latency for the user. This is ideal for optimizing performance by directing users to the closest application endpoint. Option B (Weighted routing) allows you to distribute traffic among multiple resources based on specified weights. Option C (Geolocation routing) routes traffic based on the geographic location of your users, which can be used for compliance or content localization, but latency-based routing specifically targets performance. Option D (Failover routing) is used to route traffic to a healthy resource when the primary resource becomes unhealthy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_40',
  'A developer needs to configure DNS resolution for resources within a private network across multiple AWS accounts. Which Amazon Route 53 feature allows sharing private hosted zones across accounts?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","AWS Organizations","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) is the correct service. It allows you to easily and securely share your AWS resources with any AWS account or within your AWS Organization. This includes sharing Amazon Route 53 private hosted zones, enabling cross-account DNS resolution. Option B (AWS Organizations) helps you centrally manage and govern your environment as you grow and scale your AWS resources, but it''s not the direct mechanism for sharing specific resources like private hosted zones. Option C (Amazon VPC Peering) connects two VPCs, allowing them to communicate as if they are in the same network, but it doesn''t inherently share DNS zones. Option D (AWS PrivateLink) provides private connectivity between VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications, but it''s not for sharing DNS zones.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_41',
  'Which Amazon Route 53 feature enhances the security of DNS by cryptographically signing DNS records, protecting against DNS spoofing and cache poisoning attacks?',
  'mcq',
  '["DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC)","Private Hosted Zones","Traffic Policies","Health Checks"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC)) is the correct feature. DNSSEC adds a layer of security to DNS by cryptographically signing DNS records, which helps protect against DNS spoofing and cache poisoning attacks. Option B (Private Hosted Zones) are used for DNS resolution within VPCs. Option C (Traffic Policies) allow complex routing configurations. Option D (Health Checks) monitor the health of endpoints and are used with routing policies, but they do not provide cryptographic security for DNS records.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_42',
  'A company has an on-premises DNS server and needs to resolve DNS queries for AWS private hosted zones without exposing internal resources to the public internet. Which Amazon Route 53 feature should be configured to allow this secure resolution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint","Route 53 Resolver outbound endpoint","Public Hosted Zone","Conditional forwarding on on-premises DNS"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint) is used to allow DNS queries from on-premises networks to resolve DNS records for private hosted zones in AWS. This enables secure resolution without exposing internal resources. Option B (Route 53 Resolver outbound endpoint) is used when AWS resources need to resolve DNS queries for on-premises domains. Option C (Public Hosted Zone) is for public DNS resolution and not suitable for private on-premises integration. Option D (Conditional forwarding on on-premises DNS) is part of the solution but requires a Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint in AWS to forward queries to.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_43',
  'Which Amazon Route 53 routing policy allows you to route traffic to different resources based on the user''s country, continent, or state/province?',
  'mcq',
  '["Geolocation routing","Latency-based routing","Weighted routing","Simple routing"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Geolocation routing) is specifically designed to route traffic based on the geographic location of your users, allowing you to specify different resources for different countries, continents, or even states/provinces. Option B (Latency-based routing) routes traffic to the resource with the lowest network latency. Option C (Weighted routing) distributes traffic based on assigned weights. Option D (Simple routing) is for a single resource that performs a given function for your domain.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_44',
  'A company needs to configure DNS such that a specific percentage of traffic goes to a new version of an application, while the majority goes to the old version. Which Amazon Route 53 routing policy should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["Weighted routing","Latency-based routing","Geolocation routing","Failover routing"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Weighted routing) allows you to distribute traffic among multiple resources based on specified weights. This is ideal for A/B testing or canary deployments, where a percentage of traffic is directed to a new version of an application. Option B (Latency-based routing) routes traffic based on the lowest network latency. Option C (Geolocation routing) routes traffic based on the geographic location of the user. Option D (Failover routing) is used for high availability, directing traffic to a secondary resource if the primary becomes unhealthy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_45',
  'To enable DNS resolution for on-premises clients for private hosted zones in AWS, which Amazon Route 53 component must be configured in the VPC?',
  'mcq',
  '["Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint","Route 53 Resolver outbound endpoint","Public hosted zone","VPC endpoint"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint) is the correct component. An inbound endpoint allows DNS queries from on-premises networks to be forwarded to AWS for resolution against private hosted zones and other DNS records within AWS. Option B (Route 53 Resolver outbound endpoint) is for DNS queries originating from AWS to be forwarded to on-premises DNS servers. Option C (Public hosted zone) is for public DNS resolution. Option D (VPC endpoint) provides private connectivity to AWS services or VPC endpoint services, but not specifically for on-premises DNS resolution of private hosted zones.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_46',
  'A company operates a hybrid cloud environment with resources in AWS and on-premises data centers. On-premises clients need to resolve DNS queries for private AWS resources (e.g., EC2 instances with private IPs), and AWS resources need to resolve queries for on-premises domains. The solution must provide seamless, low-latency DNS resolution without exposing internal DNS servers to the internet, ensuring secure and efficient DNS resolution across hybrid environments. Which Amazon Route 53 feature combination should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Route 53 Public Hosted Zones for both AWS and on-premises domains, and use public DNS resolvers.","Set up Route 53 Resolver Inbound Endpoints in AWS for on-premises queries and Outbound Endpoints for AWS queries to on-premises DNS servers.","Create separate Route 53 Private Hosted Zones for AWS and on-premises, and manually update records in both zones.","Use Amazon EC2 instances running BIND DNS servers in AWS and on-premises, and configure conditional forwarding between them."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most effective solution for seamless and secure hybrid DNS resolution. Route 53 Resolver Inbound Endpoints allow on-premises DNS servers to forward queries for AWS private hosted zones to AWS. Route 53 Resolver Outbound Endpoints allow AWS VPCs to forward queries for on-premises domains to on-premises DNS servers. This provides conditional forwarding, low latency, and avoids exposing internal DNS servers to the internet. Option A is incorrect because using public hosted zones for private resources is a security risk and does not provide private resolution. Option C is incorrect because manually updating records in separate zones is inefficient, error-prone, and does not provide automatic cross-environment resolution. Option D is incorrect because while technically possible, managing EC2 instances running BIND servers adds significant operational overhead compared to the managed Route 53 Resolver service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_47',
  'A large enterprise has a multi-account strategy, with separate AWS accounts for development, testing, and production environments. All applications across these accounts need to resolve internal service names defined in a central private hosted zone managed by a core networking account. The solution must enable this resolution securely and efficiently, aiming to MINIMIZE administrative overhead for DNS management across accounts. Which AWS service combination provides the best approach?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create duplicate Route 53 Private Hosted Zones in each account and synchronize records using custom scripts.","Use AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) to share the central Route 53 Private Hosted Zone from the networking account with the application accounts.","Configure Route 53 Public Hosted Zones for internal services and restrict access using security groups.","Deploy Amazon EC2 instances running DNS forwarding software in each application account to forward queries to the central networking account."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and secure way to share DNS resolution. AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) allows sharing a Route 53 Private Hosted Zone from a central account with other AWS accounts. This enables VPCs in the shared accounts to resolve records in the central private hosted zone, minimizing administrative overhead and ensuring consistency. Option A is incorrect because duplicating zones and synchronizing records is complex, error-prone, and creates significant administrative overhead. Option C is incorrect because using public hosted zones for internal services is a security anti-pattern and not suitable for private resolution. Option D is incorrect because deploying and managing EC2 instances for DNS forwarding adds operational overhead and complexity compared to the native RAM sharing mechanism.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_48',
  'A public-facing web application hosted on AWS requires enhanced security against DNS spoofing and cache poisoning attacks. The company needs to implement DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) for its domain managed in Amazon Route 53. The implementation must ensure the integrity and authenticity of DNS data. Which steps are necessary to enable DNSSEC for a domain registered with Route 53 and hosted in a Route 53 public hosted zone?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable DNSSEC signing for the public hosted zone in Route 53 and then manually upload the DS record to the domain registrar.","Configure DNSSEC validation on the client-side resolvers only, without making changes to the Route 53 hosted zone.","Use AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) to provision a DNSSEC certificate and associate it with the Route 53 hosted zone.","Migrate the domain to a third-party DNS provider that natively supports DNSSEC, as Route 53 does not support it."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes the process for enabling DNSSEC in Route 53. You must first enable DNSSEC signing for your public hosted zone within the Route 53 console or API. Route 53 then generates the necessary DNSKEY and DS records. You then take the DS record provided by Route 53 and manually upload it to your domain registrar to complete the chain of trust. Option B is incorrect because client-side validation is only effective if the DNS records themselves are signed; it doesn''t secure the authoritative DNS server. Option C is incorrect because AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) is for SSL/TLS certificates, not for DNSSEC. DNSSEC uses cryptographic keys for signing DNS records, not SSL/TLS certificates. Option D is incorrect because Amazon Route 53 does support DNSSEC for public hosted zones, making migration unnecessary.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_49',
  'A company with a hub-and-spoke network architecture across multiple AWS accounts needs to centralize DNS resolution. A central networking VPC contains Route 53 Resolver endpoints. Spoke VPCs need to forward specific domain queries to on-premises DNS servers, and on-premises servers need to resolve queries for private zones in spoke VPCs. This setup aims to centralize and simplify DNS management for complex multi-account and hybrid environments. Which Route 53 Resolver components are essential for this architecture?',
  'mcq',
  '["Only Route 53 Public Hosted Zones and Alias records.","Route 53 Resolver Inbound Endpoints, Outbound Endpoints, and Resolver Rules.","Only Route 53 Private Hosted Zones shared via AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).","Amazon EC2 instances running custom DNS forwarding software in each VPC."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the essential components. Route 53 Resolver Inbound Endpoints allow on-premises DNS servers to forward queries to AWS for private hosted zones within VPCs. Route 53 Resolver Outbound Endpoints, combined with Resolver Rules, allow VPCs to conditionally forward specific domain queries to on-premises DNS servers. This combination enables full bidirectional conditional forwarding required for complex hybrid and multi-account DNS architectures. Option A is incorrect because Public Hosted Zones are for public domains and do not facilitate private hybrid resolution. Option C is incorrect because while Private Hosted Zones shared via RAM are crucial for multi-account private DNS, they don''t inherently provide the conditional forwarding capabilities to and from on-premises networks; Resolver Endpoints are needed for that. Option D is incorrect because running custom DNS software on EC2 instances is a manual, high-overhead solution compared to the managed Route 53 Resolver service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_50',
  'A global e-commerce platform hosts its application in multiple AWS Regions (e.g., us-east-1, eu-west-1, ap-southeast-2) to serve customers worldwide. The company wants to direct users to the AWS Region that provides the lowest network latency, improving user experience. The solution must be fully managed and automatically determine the optimal region for each user. Which Amazon Route 53 routing policy should be implemented to achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["Geolocation routing policy.","Weighted routing policy.","Latency routing policy.","Failover routing policy."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is specifically designed for this scenario. A Latency routing policy directs users to the AWS Region that provides the lowest network latency from their location, based on AWS''s measurements of latency between various regions and internet endpoints. This directly optimizes user experience by reducing access times. Option A is incorrect because Geolocation routing policy routes users based on their geographic location, which might not always correspond to the lowest latency (e.g., a user in Canada might have lower latency to `us-east-1` than `us-west-2`). Option B is incorrect because Weighted routing policy distributes traffic based on specified weights, typically used for A/B testing or blue/green deployments, not for latency optimization. Option D is incorrect because Failover routing policy is used for disaster recovery, directing traffic to a secondary resource only when the primary is unhealthy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_51',
  'A security team needs to monitor all DNS queries resolved by Amazon Route 53 Resolver for auditing and threat detection purposes. They require a centralized log of all DNS queries originating from VPCs and forwarded to on-premises DNS servers, or vice-versa. The logs should be easily queryable and stored durably. Which Route 53 feature should be enabled and configured to meet this comprehensive auditing and monitoring requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable DNSSEC for all public and private hosted zones.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch Logs for VPC Flow Logs.","Enable Route 53 Resolver Query Logging and send logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs or Amazon S3.","Set up custom logging on Amazon EC2 instances running DNS servers."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the direct solution for logging DNS queries resolved by Route 53 Resolver. Route 53 Resolver Query Logging captures detailed information about DNS queries that Resolver processes, including source IP, domain name, and response. These logs can be sent to Amazon CloudWatch Logs for real-time monitoring and analysis, or to Amazon S3 for long-term archival and further processing. Option A is incorrect because DNSSEC is for securing DNS data integrity, not for logging queries. Option B is incorrect because VPC Flow Logs capture network traffic flow information (source/destination IP, port, protocol) but do not capture DNS query details. Option D is incorrect because setting up custom logging on EC2 instances running DNS servers is a manual, high-overhead approach and doesn''t cover queries resolved by the managed Route 53 Resolver service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_52',
  'A company is migrating applications from on-premises data centers to AWS, adopting a multi-account strategy. They have existing internal DNS records for their applications that need to be managed within AWS. The goal is to consolidate these records into a central Route 53 Private Hosted Zone and make them resolvable across all application VPCs in different accounts, streamlining DNS management during cloud migration and multi-account adoption. Which AWS service combination provides the most efficient solution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create a separate Route 53 Private Hosted Zone in each application account and manually replicate DNS records.","Create a single Route 53 Private Hosted Zone in a central networking account and share it with application accounts using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).","Deploy a custom DNS server on an Amazon EC2 instance in each application VPC and configure it to forward queries to a central DNS server.","Use Route 53 Public Hosted Zones for all internal DNS records and rely on security groups for access control."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and scalable solution. By creating a single Route 53 Private Hosted Zone in a central networking account and sharing it via AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM), all VPCs in the associated application accounts can resolve the internal DNS records. This centralizes management, ensures consistency, and streamlines DNS during migration. Option A is incorrect because manually replicating records across multiple private hosted zones is error-prone, time-consuming, and increases administrative overhead. Option C is incorrect because deploying and managing custom DNS servers on EC2 instances in each VPC adds significant operational burden and complexity compared to the managed Route 53 service. Option D is incorrect because using public hosted zones for internal DNS is a security anti-pattern and does not provide private resolution for internal resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_53',
  'A development team wants to perform an A/B test for a new feature of their web application. They need to direct 10% of their production traffic to a new version of the application deployed in a separate environment, while 90% continues to go to the stable version. Both versions are fronted by Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups behind Application Load Balancers. The objective is to enable controlled traffic shifting for A/B testing with minimal downtime. Which Amazon Route 53 routing policy should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Simple routing policy.","Failover routing policy.","Weighted routing policy.","Geolocation routing policy."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the ideal choice for this scenario. A Weighted routing policy in Amazon Route 53 allows you to distribute incoming traffic to multiple resources based on specified weights. By assigning a weight of 10 to the new version''s Application Load Balancer and a weight of 90 to the stable version''s Application Load Balancer, 10% of traffic will be directed to the new feature, enabling controlled A/B testing. Option A is incorrect because Simple routing policy only allows a single resource to respond to queries. Option B is incorrect because Failover routing policy is used for disaster recovery, directing traffic to a secondary resource only when the primary is unhealthy. Option D is incorrect because Geolocation routing policy routes traffic based on the user''s geographic location, which is not relevant for A/B testing across different application versions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_54',
  'Which AWS service is primarily used for managing DNS records, including public and private hosted zones, and enables conditional forwarding for complex hybrid DNS resolution scenarios between on-premises networks and AWS VPCs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon VPC","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon Route 53","AWS CloudFront"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon Route 53) is the correct service. It is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. It allows users to manage public and private hosted zones and provides features like Route 53 Resolver inbound and outbound endpoints for conditional forwarding, which is crucial for hybrid DNS resolution. Option A (Amazon VPC) provides isolated network environments in AWS but does not manage DNS records or conditional forwarding itself. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) establishes a dedicated network connection from on-premises to AWS, focusing on connectivity rather than DNS resolution. Option D (AWS CloudFront) is a content delivery network (CDN) service that caches content at edge locations, not a DNS management service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_3_55',
  'A company operates a hybrid environment with existing on-premises DNS servers and applications hosted in an AWS VPC. They need to ensure that applications in the AWS VPC can resolve DNS queries for on-premises resources, and on-premises users can resolve DNS queries for AWS resources within the VPC. Which Amazon Route 53 feature is essential to enable this bidirectional DNS resolution and conditional forwarding?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53 Public Hosted Zones","Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zones","Amazon Route 53 Resolver Endpoints","Amazon Route 53 Traffic Policies"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon Route 53 Resolver Endpoints) is the essential feature for this scenario. Route 53 Resolver provides inbound endpoints to allow on-premises DNS servers to forward queries to Route 53 for AWS resources, and outbound endpoints to allow AWS resources to forward queries to on-premises DNS servers. This enables bidirectional DNS resolution and conditional forwarding in a hybrid environment. Option A (Amazon Route 53 Public Hosted Zones) are used for public DNS resolution on the internet and are not suitable for private hybrid resolution. Option B (Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zones) are used for DNS resolution within AWS VPCs but do not inherently provide the mechanism for forwarding queries to or from on-premises networks without Resolver Endpoints. Option D (Amazon Route 53 Traffic Policies) are used to route traffic based on various criteria (e.g., latency, geolocation) for public DNS, not for hybrid DNS forwarding.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_51_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_31',
  'Which AWS service enables private connectivity between VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications without using the public internet?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront.","AWS PrivateLink.","Amazon SQS.","AWS Global Accelerator."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS PrivateLink. It provides private connectivity between VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications without exposing data to the public internet. Option A, Amazon CloudFront, is a content delivery network. Option C, Amazon SQS, is a message queuing service. Option D, AWS Global Accelerator, improves application availability and performance using the AWS global network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_32',
  'What is the main purpose of AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) in the context of VPC sharing?',
  'mcq',
  '["To automatically create and manage VPC peering connections between accounts.","To allow sharing of VPC subnets, AWS Transit Gateways, and Route 53 private hosted zones across AWS accounts.","To monitor network traffic and security groups across all shared VPCs.","To provide a centralized billing mechanism for all shared network resources."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the main purpose of AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) for VPC sharing. RAM allows you to share your AWS resources, such as VPC subnets, AWS Transit Gateways, and Route 53 private hosted zones, with other AWS accounts or within your AWS Organization. Option A is incorrect; RAM does not automatically create peering connections. Option C is a function of Amazon CloudWatch and Amazon VPC Flow Logs. Option D is related to AWS Organizations, not RAM directly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_33',
  'A company has multiple production VPCs in the same AWS Region and needs to establish full mesh connectivity between them. Which solution offers the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway.","Individual VPC peering connections between every VPC.","AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections.","AWS Direct Connect connections."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Transit Gateway. For full mesh connectivity among many VPCs, Transit Gateway acts as a central hub, significantly reducing operational overhead compared to managing a large number of individual VPC peering connections. Option B, individual VPC peering connections, would result in a complex and difficult-to-manage mesh network as the number of VPCs grows. Option C, AWS Site-to-Site VPN, and Option D, AWS Direct Connect, are primarily for connecting on-premises networks to AWS, not for inter-VPC connectivity within AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_34',
  'Which AWS service simplifies network management by acting as a central hub for connecting thousands of VPCs and on-premises networks?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon VPC.","VPC peering.","AWS Transit Gateway.","Amazon Route 53."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Transit Gateway. It serves as a central hub for connecting your VPCs and on-premises networks, simplifying network architecture and management. Option A, Amazon VPC, is a single virtual network. Option B, VPC peering, connects two VPCs directly. Option D, Amazon Route 53, is a DNS service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_35',
  'Which AWS service enables you to connect thousands of Amazon VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub, simplifying network management?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) is designed to connect thousands of Amazon VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub, significantly simplifying network management and providing a scalable solution for inter-VPC and hybrid connectivity. Option B (Amazon VPC Peering) connects two VPCs directly, which is suitable for a small number of connections but does not scale efficiently for many VPCs. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated private connection from your premises to AWS, but it''s not a central hub for VPC-to-VPC connectivity. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) creates encrypted tunnels between your on-premises network and an AWS VPC, also not a central hub for large-scale VPC connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_36',
  'A company needs to establish private connectivity between a service running in one VPC and a consumer application in another VPC, ensuring traffic does not traverse the public internet. Which AWS service should be used for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS PrivateLink","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS PrivateLink) is the correct service for establishing private connectivity between services hosted in different VPCs, or between a VPC and an AWS service, without exposing traffic to the public internet. It creates an endpoint service that consumers can connect to privately. Option B (Amazon VPC Peering) connects two VPCs directly, allowing all traffic between them, but it doesn''t offer the same service-oriented private access model as PrivateLink. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) connects multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, but PrivateLink is specifically for private service consumption. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) connects on-premises networks to VPCs over the internet, which is not suitable for private VPC-to-VPC service connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_37',
  'Which AWS service allows you to share AWS resources, such as Transit Gateways and PrivateLink endpoints, with other AWS accounts or within your AWS Organization?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","AWS Organizations","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","AWS Service Catalog"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) is the correct service. It enables you to easily and securely share your AWS resources with any AWS account or within your AWS Organization. This includes sharing Transit Gateways, PrivateLink endpoints, and other resources. Option B (AWS Organizations) helps you centrally manage and govern your environment, but RAM is the specific service for sharing resources. Option C (AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)) manages access to AWS services and resources, but it''s not for sharing resources themselves. Option D (AWS Service Catalog) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_38',
  'When connecting two Amazon VPCs directly to allow full network connectivity between them, which networking feature should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC peering connection","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (VPC peering connection) is the feature used to connect two VPCs directly, allowing them to communicate with each other as if they are within the same network. This is suitable for simple, direct connections between a limited number of VPCs. Option B (AWS Transit Gateway) is for connecting many VPCs and on-premises networks in a hub-and-spoke model. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated private connection from on-premises to AWS. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) connects on-premises networks to a VPC over the internet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_39',
  'A security team needs to monitor all network traffic flowing between VPCs connected via AWS Transit Gateway for auditing and troubleshooting. Which AWS service or feature should be enabled on the Transit Gateway attachments?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC Flow Logs","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (VPC Flow Logs) can be enabled on Transit Gateway attachments to capture detailed information about the IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC, including traffic traversing the Transit Gateway. This is crucial for security monitoring and troubleshooting. Option B (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls made to AWS services, not network traffic flow. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a logging service that can store Flow Logs, but Flow Logs themselves are the mechanism for capturing traffic. Option D (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources, not network traffic flow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_40',
  'Which AWS networking component allows you to establish a hub-and-spoke network topology, simplifying routing between multiple VPCs and on-premises networks?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","VPC Peering","Internet Gateway","NAT Gateway"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) is specifically designed to enable a hub-and-spoke network topology, acting as a central hub to connect multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, simplifying routing and management. Option B (VPC Peering) connects two VPCs directly, which is not a hub-and-spoke model. Option C (Internet Gateway) allows resources in a VPC to connect to the internet. Option D (NAT Gateway) enables private subnets to connect to the internet or other AWS services, but it does not facilitate inter-VPC or hybrid connectivity in a hub-and-spoke manner.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_41',
  'A company requires private, one-way connectivity from their applications in a VPC to a third-party service hosted in another AWS account, ensuring the service provider does not initiate connections back. Which AWS service provides this specific pattern?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS PrivateLink","VPC Peering","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Client VPN"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS PrivateLink) is ideal for this scenario. It allows consumers to access services hosted by service providers in other VPCs or AWS accounts privately, without using public IP addresses, and the connection is typically initiated by the consumer. This ensures the service provider cannot initiate connections back into the consumer''s VPC. Option B (VPC Peering) creates a full mesh connection between two VPCs, allowing bidirectional traffic, which doesn''t enforce one-way initiation. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) connects multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, but doesn''t inherently enforce one-way service consumption. Option D (AWS Client VPN) allows remote users to securely access AWS resources and on-premises networks, not for VPC-to-VPC service consumption.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_42',
  'To allow resources in a private subnet to communicate with an on-premises network connected via AWS Transit Gateway, what must be configured in the private subnet''s route table?',
  'mcq',
  '["A route to the Transit Gateway","A route to an Internet Gateway","A route to a NAT Gateway","A route to a VPC endpoint"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (A route to the Transit Gateway) is necessary. For resources in a private subnet to communicate with an on-premises network through a Transit Gateway, the private subnet''s route table must have a route that directs traffic destined for the on-premises network CIDR block to the Transit Gateway. Option B (A route to an Internet Gateway) is for internet access, not on-premises connectivity. Option C (A route to a NAT Gateway) is for outbound internet access from private subnets, not for on-premises connectivity. Option D (A route to a VPC endpoint) is for private access to AWS services or endpoint services, not for general on-premises network routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_43',
  'A large enterprise operates dozens of VPCs across several AWS accounts and Regions. Each VPC needs to communicate with a shared services VPC and potentially with other application VPCs. The current setup uses a mesh of VPC peering connections, which is becoming unmanageable and difficult to scale due to the N(N-1)/2 connections required. The company needs to SIMPLIFY and scale inter-VPC connectivity across accounts and Regions. Which AWS networking service provides the most efficient and scalable solution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue using VPC peering connections and manage them with a custom script.","Deploy an AWS Transit Gateway in a central networking account and connect all VPCs to it.","Use AWS Direct Connect to route traffic between all VPCs through an on-premises network.","Implement AWS PrivateLink for all inter-VPC communication."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and scalable solution. AWS Transit Gateway acts as a central hub for connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks. It simplifies the network architecture by reducing the number of connections from a mesh (N*N-1/2) to a hub-and-spoke model, making it highly manageable and scalable across accounts and Regions. Option A is incorrect because VPC peering becomes unmanageable and difficult to scale with a large number of VPCs due to the quadratic increase in connections. Option C is incorrect because routing all inter-VPC traffic through an on-premises network (hairpinning) introduces unnecessary latency, complexity, and single points of failure, and is not designed for inter-VPC communication. Option D is incorrect because AWS PrivateLink is designed for private, one-to-one connectivity to specific services, not for general-purpose inter-VPC routing between many VPCs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_44',
  'A SaaS provider hosts its service in a dedicated AWS VPC and needs to offer private, secure access to its customers, who also have AWS VPCs. Customers require that traffic to the SaaS service does not traverse the public internet, and the SaaS provider wants to manage access centrally. The solution must provide secure, private, and scalable access to a SaaS service without public internet exposure. Which AWS service combination should the SaaS provider implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a public Application Load Balancer for the SaaS service and rely on customer VPNs for secure access.","Create an AWS PrivateLink VPC Endpoint Service for the SaaS service and have customers create VPC Endpoints in their VPCs.","Establish VPC peering connections between the SaaS VPC and each customer''s VPC.","Deploy a NAT Gateway in the SaaS VPC to route traffic privately to customer VPCs."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the ideal solution. AWS PrivateLink allows the SaaS provider to create a VPC Endpoint Service, exposing their service privately. Customers can then create a VPC Endpoint in their own VPCs, establishing a private connection to the SaaS service without traversing the public internet. This offers secure, scalable, and simplified access. Option A is incorrect because using a public Application Load Balancer exposes the service to the internet, which violates the requirement for private access. Option C is incorrect because VPC peering connections are not scalable for a large number of customers and require managing a mesh of connections. Option D is incorrect because a NAT Gateway is used for instances in private subnets to access the internet, not for providing private access to a service from other VPCs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_45',
  'A company has a central networking account where an AWS Transit Gateway is deployed. Multiple application teams, each with their own AWS account and VPCs, need to connect their VPCs to this central Transit Gateway to enable communication with shared services and on-premises networks. The objective is to facilitate secure and managed sharing of network infrastructure across organizational units. Which AWS service is essential for enabling these cross-account Transit Gateway attachments?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Organizations for account management.","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) for sharing the Transit Gateway.","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) for cross-account roles.","AWS PrivateLink for private service access."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the essential service. AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) allows you to share AWS resources, including AWS Transit Gateways, with other AWS accounts or within your AWS Organization. This enables application teams in different accounts to attach their VPCs to the central Transit Gateway, facilitating cross-account connectivity. Option A is incorrect because while AWS Organizations helps manage multiple accounts, it doesn''t directly facilitate the sharing of network resources like Transit Gateway attachments. Option C is incorrect because while IAM roles are used for permissions, RAM is the specific service for sharing the resource itself. Option D is incorrect because AWS PrivateLink is for private access to specific services, not for general-purpose network infrastructure sharing like Transit Gateway.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_46',
  'An organization wants to establish a hub-and-spoke network topology where a central AWS Transit Gateway acts as the hub, connecting numerous spoke VPCs in different accounts. This design aims to simplify routing, centralize network security appliances, and provide controlled communication between spokes. The primary goal is to establish a scalable and manageable network topology for inter-VPC communication. Which key configuration element within Transit Gateway is crucial for controlling traffic flow between attached VPCs?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC peering connections between all spoke VPCs.","Separate Transit Gateway route tables associated with different VPC attachments.","Network ACLs configured on each Transit Gateway attachment.","Security groups applied directly to the Transit Gateway."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is crucial for controlling traffic flow. AWS Transit Gateway allows you to create multiple Transit Gateway route tables and associate different VPC attachments with specific route tables. This enables fine-grained control over which VPCs can communicate with each other (e.g., isolating development VPCs from production, or forcing traffic through a security appliance VPC). Option A is incorrect because VPC peering connections are what Transit Gateway aims to replace for scalability and manageability in a hub-and-spoke model. Option C is incorrect because Network ACLs operate at the subnet level within a VPC, not directly on Transit Gateway attachments for inter-VPC routing control. Option D is incorrect because security groups are applied to network interfaces within a VPC, not directly to the Transit Gateway itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_47',
  'A security team needs to gain deep visibility into all network traffic flowing between VPCs connected to a central AWS Transit Gateway. They require a mechanism to capture and analyze flow records for auditing, troubleshooting, and anomaly detection, ensuring comprehensive monitoring and visibility of inter-VPC traffic for security and operational insights. Which AWS service should be enabled and configured on the Transit Gateway to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail for API activity logging.","Amazon CloudWatch for Transit Gateway metrics.","VPC Flow Logs enabled on Transit Gateway attachments.","AWS Config rules to monitor Transit Gateway configurations."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the direct solution for capturing network traffic flow. VPC Flow Logs can be enabled on Transit Gateway attachments to capture detailed information about IP traffic going to and from the Transit Gateway. These logs can then be published to Amazon CloudWatch Logs or Amazon S3 for analysis, auditing, and troubleshooting. Option A is incorrect because AWS CloudTrail logs API calls made to AWS services, not network traffic flow. Option B is incorrect because Amazon CloudWatch provides metrics (e.g., bytes in/out) but not detailed flow records. Option D is incorrect because AWS Config monitors resource configurations for compliance, not real-time network traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_48',
  'A large application within a single AWS account consists of multiple microservices deployed in different VPCs. One critical microservice needs to be accessed privately by other microservices without exposing it to the public internet or traversing VPC peering connections. The access should be simplified and managed, enabling secure and simplified private access between internal microservices in different VPCs within the same account. Which AWS service is designed for this specific private connectivity use case?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway.","Amazon VPC Peering.","AWS PrivateLink (VPC Endpoint Service and VPC Endpoint).","AWS Direct Connect."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is specifically designed for this use case. AWS PrivateLink allows you to expose a service (e.g., a microservice behind a Network Load Balancer) as a VPC Endpoint Service. Other VPCs can then create a VPC Endpoint to privately access this service as if it were in their own VPC, without traversing the public internet or requiring complex VPC peering configurations. Option A is incorrect because AWS Transit Gateway is for general-purpose inter-VPC routing, not for exposing a specific service privately. Option B is incorrect because VPC peering connects entire VPCs, which can be overly permissive and complex to manage for service-specific access. Option D is incorrect because AWS Direct Connect is for connecting on-premises networks to AWS, not for inter-VPC communication within AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_49',
  'A company initially connected two development VPCs using VPC peering. Now, they are expanding to include five more VPCs across different accounts, all needing to communicate with each other and a central shared services VPC. The current peering setup is becoming complex to manage and scale. The company needs to choose the MOST scalable and manageable solution for growing inter-VPC connectivity. Which AWS networking service should they migrate to?',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue with VPC peering, adding new connections as needed, and manage them with custom scripts.","Implement AWS PrivateLink for all inter-VPC communication.","Migrate to AWS Transit Gateway to centralize connectivity.","Use AWS Direct Connect to route all inter-VPC traffic through an on-premises data center."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most scalable and manageable solution. AWS Transit Gateway is designed to simplify network architecture by acting as a central hub for connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks. It eliminates the need for a mesh of VPC peering connections, which becomes unmanageable as the number of VPCs grows (N*(N-1)/2 connections). Option A is incorrect because continuing with VPC peering will exacerbate the management and scalability issues. Option B is incorrect because AWS PrivateLink is for private, one-to-one access to specific services, not for general-purpose inter-VPC routing between many VPCs. Option D is incorrect because routing inter-VPC traffic through an on-premises data center via Direct Connect (hairpinning) introduces unnecessary latency, complexity, and cost, and is not its intended use case.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_50',
  'Which AWS service acts as a central network hub to connect multiple Amazon VPCs, AWS accounts, and on-premises networks, simplifying network management and reducing the need for numerous point-to-point connections?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) is the correct service. It acts as a network transit hub, allowing you to connect thousands of VPCs and on-premises networks to a single gateway. This simplifies network topology, centralizes routing, and eliminates complex mesh connections. Option B (Amazon VPC Peering) creates a direct network connection between two VPCs, but it''s a point-to-point solution that doesn''t scale well for many VPCs or provide a central hub. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated private connection from on-premises to AWS, focusing on the last mile connectivity rather than inter-VPC routing. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) establishes an encrypted connection over the public internet between an on-premises network and a VPC, which is also a point-to-point connection and not a central hub for multiple VPCs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_51_m_51_2_51',
  'A company has a microservices application deployed in a VPC in Account A, and a critical shared service (e.g., a database or API) hosted in another VPC in Account B. The application in Account A needs to securely access the shared service in Account B without traversing the public internet, exposing the service publicly, or requiring complex VPC peering relationships. Which AWS service provides the MOST secure and scalable solution for this private connectivity?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","Internet Gateway"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS PrivateLink) is the most secure and scalable solution. It enables private connectivity between VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications without exposing data to the public internet. It creates an interface VPC endpoint in the consumer''s VPC, allowing access to the service in the provider''s VPC as if it were hosted locally, without requiring VPC peering or route table management for each connection. Option A (Amazon VPC Peering) creates a direct network connection between two VPCs but requires managing routing tables in both VPCs and doesn''t provide the same level of service isolation as PrivateLink, especially for shared services across many consumers. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) is used for centralizing routing across many VPCs and on-premises networks, but it''s a routing solution, not specifically designed for privately exposing a service to multiple consumers without public exposure. Option D (Internet Gateway) is incorrect as it connects a VPC to the public internet, which violates the requirement of not exposing the service publicly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain51_11',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_51_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

