) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_4_30',
  'A development team is deploying a new application that processes sensitive financial transactions. They need to monitor the application''s performance and resource utilization in real-time, trigger automated actions when specific thresholds are breached, and store historical metrics for auditing. Which AWS services are most appropriate for achieving these monitoring and alerting objectives? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch Metrics","Amazon CloudWatch Alarms","AWS Config","Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Metrics) collects and processes raw data from AWS services into readable, near real-time metrics, which is fundamental for monitoring application performance and resource utilization. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Alarms) allows users to set thresholds on these metrics and trigger automated actions (like sending notifications or scaling resources) when those thresholds are violated. Option A (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events, primarily for auditing and governance, not real-time performance monitoring. Option D (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of AWS resources, focusing on compliance, not real-time operational metrics. Option E (Amazon S3) is an object storage service, not directly used for real-time monitoring or alarming.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_4_31',
  'A company is migrating its legacy applications to AWS and needs to ensure that all network traffic within their VPCs is continuously monitored for anomalous behavior and potential security threats. They also require detailed logs of all API calls made to their AWS resources for auditing and compliance purposes. Which two AWS services are essential for fulfilling these monitoring and auditing requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS CloudTrail","VPC Flow Logs","AWS Config","Amazon GuardDuty"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (AWS CloudTrail) provides a record of API calls and related events made by a user, role, or an AWS service in AWS. This is crucial for auditing and compliance. Option C (VPC Flow Logs) captures information about the IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC, which is vital for monitoring network traffic for anomalous behavior and security threats. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a service for centralizing logs, which can be used to store CloudTrail logs and VPC Flow Logs, but CloudTrail and Flow Logs are the source of the audit and network traffic data. Option D (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources, focusing on compliance of resource configurations rather than API activity or network traffic. Option E (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, often consuming data from CloudTrail and VPC Flow Logs, but it''s a detection service, not the primary logging/auditing source for API calls or network traffic itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_4_32',
  'A development team needs to monitor the performance of their serverless application built with AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. They require metrics on invocation counts, errors, and latency, as well as the ability to create custom dashboards to visualize these metrics. Additionally, they want to receive notifications when specific error rates exceed a defined threshold. Which two AWS services are fundamental for this monitoring and alerting setup? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS X-Ray","Amazon CloudWatch Metrics","Amazon CloudWatch Alarms","AWS Config","Amazon SNS"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Metrics) automatically collects metrics from AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway, providing data on invocation counts, errors, and latency. These metrics can be visualized on custom dashboards. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Alarms) allows users to define thresholds for these metrics and trigger actions, such as sending notifications via Amazon SNS (Option E) when an error rate exceeds a specified limit. Option A (AWS X-Ray) is for distributed tracing and analyzing application performance, which is complementary but not the primary service for collecting basic metrics and setting alarms. Option D (AWS Config) is for compliance and configuration management, not real-time performance monitoring. While Amazon SNS (Option E) is used for notifications, CloudWatch Alarms is the service that *triggers* those notifications based on metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_4_33',
  'A development team is building a new application that processes real-time data streams using AWS Lambda. They need to ensure that the Lambda functions are invoked by an event source that can buffer and process large volumes of data, and that the invocation is asynchronous to avoid blocking the upstream service. Which two AWS services are suitable for triggering AWS Lambda functions in this event-driven, asynchronous manner? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SQS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon SNS","Amazon S3","Amazon DynamoDB"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service that can buffer messages and trigger Lambda functions asynchronously, providing decoupling between producers and consumers. Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is a real-time data streaming service that can collect and process large streams of data, and can be configured as an event source to trigger Lambda functions asynchronously. Option C (Amazon SNS) is a notification service that can trigger Lambda functions, but it''s primarily for fan-out messaging, not for buffering large volumes of data streams like SQS or Kinesis. Option D (Amazon S3) can trigger Lambda functions on object events (e.g., object creation), but it''s object storage, not a real-time data stream or message queue. Option E (Amazon DynamoDB) can trigger Lambda functions via DynamoDB Streams for changes in table items, but it''s a NoSQL database, not a general-purpose message queue or data stream for external events.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_4_34',
  'A development team is building a new application that will process large volumes of streaming data from IoT devices. They need a service that can ingest and store this data in real-time, and then trigger serverless functions for immediate processing. The solution must be highly scalable and durable. Which two AWS services are best suited for this real-time data ingestion and processing pipeline? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SQS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","AWS Lambda","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is designed for real-time ingestion and processing of large streams of data, making it ideal for IoT device data. It provides high throughput and durability. Option C (AWS Lambda) can be directly triggered by Kinesis Data Streams to process records as they arrive, providing serverless, event-driven processing without managing servers. Option A (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service, suitable for asynchronous processing but not optimized for continuous, high-volume data streams like Kinesis. Option D (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database, not a streaming ingestion service. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is a serverless event bus for routing events between services, but Kinesis Data Streams is more appropriate for raw data ingestion.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_4_35',
  'A company is building a serverless application using AWS Lambda and needs to store configuration data and user preferences. This data needs to be highly available, durable, and accessible with low latency. Additionally, they require a mechanism to trigger Lambda functions whenever there are changes to specific data items. Which two AWS services are best suited for storing this data and enabling event-driven processing based on data changes? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon RDS","Amazon DynamoDB Streams","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Amazon DynamoDB) is a fully managed NoSQL database that provides high availability, durability, and low-latency access, making it ideal for storing configuration data and user preferences for serverless applications. Option D (Amazon DynamoDB Streams) captures a time-ordered sequence of item-level modifications in a DynamoDB table and can be configured as an event source to trigger AWS Lambda functions, enabling event-driven processing based on data changes. Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage, not typically used for structured configuration data that needs item-level change notifications. Option C (Amazon RDS) is a relational database, which might be overkill and less ''serverless-native'' for simple configuration and preferences. Option E (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) is good for storing configuration parameters, but it doesn''t provide item-level change streams to trigger Lambda functions in the same way DynamoDB Streams does.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

