INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_21',
  'Which AWS networking constructs are suitable for connecting two VPCs in the same AWS Region with low latency? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'VPC Peering (Option A) allows you to connect two VPCs directly, enabling them to communicate as if they are on the same network, offering low latency. AWS Transit Gateway (Option B) can also connect VPCs within the same Region, providing a central hub for routing, which can also offer low latency. AWS Direct Connect (Option C) and AWS Site-to-Site VPN (Option D) are for hybrid connectivity to on-premises networks. Amazon Route 53 (Option E) is a DNS service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_22',
  'Which AWS services can help manage and centralize network routing across a complex multi-VPC, multi-account environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Network Manager","Amazon Route 53","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Organizations"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'AWS Transit Gateway (Option A) acts as a central hub for connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, simplifying routing. AWS Network Manager (Option B) provides a single operational dashboard and monitoring for your global network, including Transit Gateways and VPN connections. Amazon Route 53 (Option C) is a DNS service. AWS CloudFormation (Option D) is for infrastructure as code. AWS Organizations (Option E) helps manage multiple AWS accounts but doesn''t directly manage network routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_23',
  'A company needs to connect several VPCs located in different AWS accounts within the same AWS Region, creating a hub-and-spoke topology. They also want to share common network services. Which AWS services are most suitable for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","VPC Peering","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","AWS VPN","Amazon CloudFront"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) is ideal for a hub-and-spoke topology, acting as a central hub to connect multiple VPCs across different accounts and simplifying routing. Option C (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) allows you to share your Transit Gateway with other AWS accounts, enabling the hub-and-spoke model across an organization. While VPC Peering (Option B) can connect VPCs, it becomes complex to manage with many VPCs in a full mesh. AWS VPN (Option D) is for connecting on-premises networks or individual clients, not primarily for inter-VPC connectivity within AWS. Amazon CloudFront (Option E) is a content delivery network service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_24',
  'A solutions architect is designing a multi-account, multi-Region architecture where applications in different VPCs need to communicate privately and securely without exposing traffic to the public internet. Which AWS services enable this private and secure cross-VPC and cross-Region communication? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","AWS PrivateLink","Internet Gateway","NAT Gateway","VPC Endpoints"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) provides a central hub for connecting VPCs across accounts and Regions, facilitating private and secure communication. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) allows you to privately connect your VPCs to services hosted by AWS or other AWS accounts, without using an Internet Gateway, VPC peering connections, or Direct Connect. VPC Endpoints (Option E) are a component of PrivateLink, specifically for connecting to AWS services. Internet Gateway (Option C) provides internet access, which is contrary to the requirement for private communication. NAT Gateway (Option D) allows instances in a private subnet to connect to the internet, but not for private inter-VPC communication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_25',
  'A company has several VPCs in different AWS accounts that need to communicate. Some of these VPCs have overlapping IP address ranges, which prevents direct VPC peering. Which AWS services or approaches can help establish connectivity in this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway with network address translation (NAT) appliances","VPC Peering with IP address re-architecture","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway with network address translation (NAT) appliances) is a viable solution. While Transit Gateway itself doesn''t solve IP overlap, you can deploy NAT appliances (e.g., EC2 instances running NAT software) in a shared services VPC attached to the Transit Gateway to translate overlapping IPs. Option B (VPC Peering with IP address re-architecture) is a direct solution; if IP ranges overlap, the only way to use VPC peering is to re-architect the IP addressing to eliminate the overlap. Option C (AWS PrivateLink) is for private access to specific services, not general VPC-to-VPC routing with overlapping IPs. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) and Option E (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) connect on-premises networks to AWS, not primarily for resolving overlapping IPs between VPCs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_26',
  'A solutions architect needs to design a network architecture that allows resources in one VPC to access services in another VPC in a different AWS account, ensuring that the traffic remains within the AWS network and does not traverse the public internet. Which AWS services can achieve this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","Internet Gateway","NAT Gateway","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (VPC Peering) allows you to connect two VPCs directly, enabling private communication between them using AWS''s private network infrastructure. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) enables private connectivity between VPCs and services hosted by AWS or other AWS accounts, ensuring traffic stays within the AWS network. Internet Gateway (Option C) provides internet access, which is explicitly against the requirement. NAT Gateway (Option D) allows private subnets to initiate outbound connections to the internet. AWS Global Accelerator (Option E) routes user traffic to optimal endpoints over the AWS global network but is not for private inter-VPC communication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_27',
  'Which AWS services can be used to centralize routing for multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, simplifying network management and enabling inter-VPC communication across different accounts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","VPC Peering","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) acts as a central network hub, allowing you to connect thousands of VPCs and on-premises networks across multiple accounts and Regions, simplifying routing and management. Option B (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) allows you to share your Transit Gateway with other AWS accounts within your AWS Organization, enabling centralized routing across accounts. VPC Peering (Option C) connects two VPCs directly, which becomes complex to manage in a large-scale, multi-VPC environment. AWS Direct Connect (Option D) connects on-premises networks to AWS, but doesn''t centralize inter-VPC routing. Amazon Route 53 (Option E) is a DNS service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_28',
  'A global company operates applications across several AWS Regions and multiple AWS accounts. They need to establish a scalable and secure network architecture that allows VPCs in different accounts and Regions to communicate efficiently, while also providing a centralized point for network inspection and simplified routing. Which two AWS services or features should be foundational to this design? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) is ideal for creating a hub-and-spoke network architecture that connects multiple VPCs across different accounts and Regions, simplifying routing and enabling centralized network inspection. Option D (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) is crucial for sharing AWS Transit Gateways (and other resources) across different AWS accounts, allowing the centralized network infrastructure to be used by various application teams without duplication. Option A (VPC Peering) is suitable for connecting a small number of VPCs but becomes unmanageable and unscalable for a large number of VPCs across many accounts. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) provides private connectivity to services, but not general-purpose VPC-to-VPC or cross-Region connectivity. Option E (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service, not a network connectivity service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_29',
  'A software-as-a-service (SaaS) provider hosts its application in a central AWS VPC and needs to offer private, secure access to its service for customers in their own separate AWS VPCs, without exposing the service to the public internet or requiring complex VPC peering configurations. The solution must minimize network management overhead for both the provider and the consumers. Which two AWS services or features are best suited for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zones","VPC Endpoints"]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (AWS PrivateLink) allows the SaaS provider to create an endpoint service that customers can connect to privately from their own VPCs using VPC Endpoints, without exposing the service to the public internet. Option E (VPC Endpoints) are the consumer-side component of PrivateLink, enabling customers to establish private connections to the SaaS provider''s service. Option A (VPC Peering) would require managing many-to-many connections, which is complex and doesn''t scale well for a SaaS offering. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) can connect many VPCs but is a broader networking solution, not specifically designed for private service exposure in a SaaS model. Option D (Amazon Route 53 Private Hosted Zones) is for private DNS resolution within VPCs, not for establishing private network connectivity to a service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_30',
  'A large enterprise needs to centralize network traffic inspection for all inter-VPC communication within their AWS environment, spanning multiple accounts and Regions. They want to route all East-West traffic through a dedicated security VPC for deep packet inspection by third-party virtual appliances. Which two AWS networking services are fundamental to implementing this centralized inspection architecture? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) is crucial for building a hub-and-spoke network architecture, allowing all VPCs to connect to a central Transit Gateway, which can then route traffic through a security VPC for inspection. Option D (Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)) is specifically designed to distribute traffic to a fleet of virtual appliances (like firewalls or intrusion detection systems) in a transparent manner, making it ideal for centralizing network inspection within a security VPC. Option A (VPC Peering) is not scalable for a large number of VPCs and does not easily facilitate centralized traffic inspection. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) provides private connectivity to services, not general-purpose inter-VPC routing for inspection. Option E (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service, not a networking service for traffic routing or inspection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_31',
  'A large enterprise has a multi-account AWS environment with several VPCs in different accounts that need to communicate with each other. They want to avoid a mesh of VPC peering connections and instead establish a scalable hub-and-spoke network topology. Additionally, they need to ensure that network resources, such as the central hub, can be easily shared and managed across these accounts. Which two AWS services are fundamental to this architectural approach? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) is designed to simplify network topology by acting as a central hub for connecting multiple VPCs across different accounts and Regions in a hub-and-spoke model, eliminating the need for a complex mesh of VPC peering connections. Option D (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) enables the sharing of AWS Transit Gateways (and other resources) across different AWS accounts, which is crucial for a centralized network team to manage the hub while application teams in other accounts connect their VPCs. Option A (VPC Peering) is suitable for connecting a small number of VPCs but does not scale well for a large enterprise with many accounts. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) provides private connectivity to services, not general-purpose VPC-to-VPC connectivity. Option E (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service, not a network connectivity service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_32',
  'A company is expanding its AWS footprint to multiple Regions to improve disaster recovery capabilities and reduce latency for global users. They need to ensure that VPCs in different Regions can communicate securely and efficiently, without routing traffic over the public internet. Which two AWS networking constructs are best suited for establishing robust cross-Region connectivity? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon CloudFront"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (VPC Peering) can be established between VPCs in different AWS Regions to enable direct, private communication between them. While it works for a few connections, it can become complex to manage at scale. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) supports inter-Region peering, allowing Transit Gateways in different Regions to connect, thereby enabling VPCs attached to those Transit Gateways to communicate across Regions. This provides a more scalable and manageable solution for cross-Region connectivity compared to a mesh of VPC peering connections. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) provides private connectivity to services, not general-purpose VPC-to-VPC or cross-Region connectivity. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) provides private connectivity from on-premises to AWS, not between AWS Regions. Option E (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network, not a service for direct VPC-to-VPC communication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_33',
  'A large organization has a complex AWS environment with multiple VPCs in different accounts, and they need to simplify inter-VPC communication while maintaining network segmentation and security. They want to avoid a full mesh of VPC peering connections and instead use a centralized routing solution. Additionally, they need to share common network services, such as a centralized firewall VPC, across all connected VPCs. Which two AWS services are best suited for this architecture? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","Amazon VPC NAT Gateway"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) is ideal for creating a hub-and-spoke network architecture that connects multiple VPCs across different accounts, simplifying inter-VPC communication and providing a centralized point for routing and inspection. Option D (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) enables the sharing of AWS Transit Gateways (and other resources) across different AWS accounts, allowing a centralized network team to manage the Transit Gateway while application teams in other accounts connect their VPCs and utilize shared network services like a firewall VPC. Option A (VPC Peering) is not scalable for a large number of VPCs and accounts. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) provides private connectivity to services, not general-purpose VPC-to-VPC routing. Option E (Amazon VPC NAT Gateway) provides outbound internet access for private subnets, not inter-VPC routing or resource sharing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_50_m_50_6_34',
  'A large enterprise with a multi-account AWS environment needs to establish secure and scalable connectivity between VPCs owned by different departments, which reside in separate AWS accounts. They want to avoid creating a complex mesh of individual VPC peering connections and instead centralize their network routing. Additionally, they need to share network resources, such as a centralized DNS resolver, across these connected VPCs. Which two AWS services are critical for this architecture? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","Amazon Route 53 Resolver"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) is ideal for creating a hub-and-spoke network architecture, connecting multiple VPCs across different accounts and simplifying routing. This avoids the complexity of a full mesh of VPC peering connections. Option D (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) enables the sharing of AWS Transit Gateways (and other resources like Route 53 Resolver rules or endpoints) across different AWS accounts, allowing centralized management of network infrastructure while enabling various departments to connect their VPCs. Option A (VPC Peering) is not scalable for many VPCs and accounts. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) provides private connectivity to services, not general-purpose VPC-to-VPC connectivity. Option E (Amazon Route 53 Resolver) is for DNS resolution, which can be shared via RAM, but Transit Gateway and RAM are the core services for the connectivity and sharing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain50_11',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_50_6',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

