INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_49',
  'A global financial institution is deploying a new trading application in AWS, requiring ultra-low latency and high-throughput connectivity to its on-premises data centers via AWS Direct Connect. The network team has established multiple Direct Connect connections to different AWS Regions and needs to ensure that specific critical application traffic always egresses AWS towards the on-premises network through the lowest latency path, even if other paths have a shorter BGP AS_PATH. Which BGP attribute, when configured on the AWS side, can be used to influence AWS''s outbound routing preference for this critical traffic?',
  'mcq',
  '["BGP Local Preference","BGP AS_PATH Prepending","BGP Multi-Exit Discriminator (MED)","BGP Community Strings"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (BGP Local Preference) is the correct answer. BGP Local Preference is a well-known discretionary BGP attribute that is exchanged within an Autonomous System (AS) to influence the best path selection for outbound traffic. When configured on the AWS side (e.g., through a Direct Connect Gateway route policy or a Transit Gateway route table), it can be used to make AWS prefer a specific Direct Connect connection or VPN tunnel for traffic destined for on-premises networks, overriding other BGP path selection criteria like AS_PATH length. This allows the financial institution to direct critical application traffic over the desired low-latency path. Option B (BGP AS_PATH Prepending) is used by the advertising router to make its path appear longer to the receiving router, thereby influencing the receiving router to choose an alternative path. This is typically used by the on-premises network to influence AWS''s inbound traffic, not by AWS to influence its own outbound traffic preference. Option C (BGP Multi-Exit Discriminator (MED)) is used to influence how traffic enters an AS when there are multiple entry points from a neighboring AS. AWS considers MED values only if multiple routes for the same prefix are received from the *same* Direct Connect connection or VPN connection, and it has a lower precedence than Local Preference in AWS''s path selection. Option D (BGP Community Strings) are used for tagging routes and can be used for various purposes, including filtering or setting local preference on the receiving side. While AWS supports specific BGP communities for Direct Connect, Local Preference is the direct attribute for influencing AWS''s outbound path selection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_50',
  'A multinational corporation operates a hybrid cloud environment, connecting its on-premises data centers in multiple continents to AWS using a combination of AWS Direct Connect and AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections. The company requires a highly resilient and cost-effective solution for inter-Region communication between its AWS VPCs, which also need to exchange traffic with on-premises networks. The solution must minimize the number of network appliances managed by the customer and simplify routing policies. Which AWS networking construct should be leveraged to achieve this with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Multiple VPC Peering connections between all VPCs and dedicated VPNs to each on-premises site.","AWS Transit Gateway with Direct Connect Gateway and Site-to-Site VPN attachments.","Individual VPN connections from each VPC to a central on-premises router.","A mesh of VPN connections between all VPCs and all on-premises sites for redundancy."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Transit Gateway with Direct Connect Gateway and Site-to-Site VPN attachments) is the most suitable solution for achieving highly resilient and cost-effective inter-Region and hybrid cloud connectivity with the LEAST operational overhead. AWS Transit Gateway acts as a central hub, simplifying network architecture by allowing VPCs and on-premises networks (via Direct Connect Gateway and VPN) to connect to a single gateway. This eliminates the need for a complex mesh of peering connections or individual VPNs, significantly reducing management complexity and operational overhead. Option A (Multiple VPC Peering connections between all VPCs and dedicated VPNs to each on-premises site) would result in a complex and unmanageable mesh of connections, especially as the number of VPCs grows, increasing operational overhead and making routing difficult to manage. Option C (Individual VPN connections from each VPC to a central on-premises router) would also lead to a scaling challenge, requiring many VPN connections and complex routing configurations on both AWS and on-premises, increasing operational burden and potential for misconfiguration. Option D (A mesh of VPN connections between all VPCs and all on-premises sites for redundancy) is an anti-pattern that would be extremely difficult to implement, manage, and scale, leading to the highest operational overhead and significant cost.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_51',
  'A large enterprise has deployed a complex multi-VPC architecture across several AWS Regions, all connected via AWS Transit Gateway. The security team mandates strict network segmentation, requiring that traffic between specific VPCs must traverse a dedicated Network Firewall VPC for inspection, while other VPCs can communicate directly. The network administrator needs to configure the Transit Gateway to enforce these routing policies dynamically and with minimal manual intervention. Which Transit Gateway feature enables this advanced routing control?',
  'mcq',
  '["Static Routes on Transit Gateway attachments","Transit Gateway Route Table Associations and Propagations","Transit Gateway Network Manager","VPC Peering with Transit Gateway"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Transit Gateway Route Table Associations and Propagations) is the correct answer. AWS Transit Gateway allows the creation of multiple route tables. By associating VPC attachments with specific Transit Gateway route tables and propagating routes from other attachments to these tables, an administrator can implement complex routing policies. For instance, a firewall VPC attachment can be associated with a dedicated route table, and other VPCs can propagate their routes to it, while also associating with a route table that directs traffic through the firewall for inspection. This dynamic and granular control over traffic flow is central to implementing network segmentation and security policies with Transit Gateway. Option A (Static Routes on Transit Gateway attachments) is useful for specific, unchanging routes but does not offer the dynamic, policy-driven segmentation required across multiple VPCs and for enforcing traffic inspection through a central firewall VPC. Option C (Transit Gateway Network Manager) is a monitoring and management tool for Transit Gateway and hybrid networks, not a feature for configuring routing policies within the Transit Gateway itself. Option D (VPC Peering with Transit Gateway) is not a feature; VPC peering is a separate connection method, and while Transit Gateway can connect VPCs, the routing policies are managed via its route tables, not by ''VPC Peering with Transit Gateway''.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_52',
  'A SaaS provider offers a service that processes sensitive customer data and needs to allow its enterprise customers to securely connect their AWS VPCs to the SaaS application without exposing the application to the public internet or requiring complex network configurations like VPC peering or VPNs for each customer. The solution must ensure that customer traffic remains on the AWS network and is isolated from other customers'' traffic, while also minimizing the operational burden on both the SaaS provider and its customers. Which AWS service provides the MOST secure and operationally efficient solution for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Client VPN endpoint for each customer VPC.","Internet-facing Application Load Balancer with IP whitelisting.","AWS PrivateLink with a VPC Endpoint Service.","AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections between each customer VPC and the SaaS VPC."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS PrivateLink with a VPC Endpoint Service) is the most secure and operationally efficient solution. AWS PrivateLink allows customers to connect their VPCs to services hosted by other AWS accounts (service providers) without using public IPs, VPC peering, or VPNs. The SaaS provider creates a VPC Endpoint Service, and customers create a VPC Endpoint in their VPCs, establishing a private, secure, one-way connection. Traffic flows entirely within the AWS network, enhancing security and simplifying network management for both parties. Option A (AWS Client VPN endpoint for each customer VPC) is designed for remote access for individual users, not for VPC-to-VPC service consumption, and would be operationally complex and costly to manage for multiple enterprise customers. Option B (Internet-facing Application Load Balancer with IP whitelisting) exposes the service to the public internet, which is less secure and requires customers to manage IP whitelisting, increasing operational burden and security risks. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections between each customer VPC and the SaaS VPC) would create a complex mesh of VPNs, leading to significant operational overhead, management complexity, and potential for routing conflicts as the number of customers grows.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_53',
  'An organization with a global presence utilizes AWS Direct Connect to establish private connectivity between its on-premises data centers and multiple AWS Regions. They have several VPCs in different Regions that need to communicate with each other and with the on-premises networks through a centralized, resilient, and high-bandwidth connection. The current architecture involves multiple individual Direct Connect connections and complex routing. Which AWS service is specifically designed to centralize Direct Connect connections and facilitate inter-Region VPC communication over these connections?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect Gateway","AWS Transit Gateway","VPC Peering","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Direct Connect Gateway) is the correct answer. A Direct Connect Gateway is a globally available resource that allows you to connect your Direct Connect connections to one or more VPCs in any supported AWS Region, as long as the VPCs are associated with a Transit Gateway. It centralizes your Direct Connect connections, enabling private connectivity to multiple VPCs across different Regions using a single Direct Connect connection. This simplifies network architecture and routing compared to managing separate connections for each Region or VPC. Option B (AWS Transit Gateway) is used to connect multiple VPCs and on-premises networks (via Direct Connect Gateway or VPN) within and across Regions, but the Direct Connect Gateway is the specific component that centralizes the Direct Connect connections themselves. Option C (VPC Peering) allows direct network connectivity between two VPCs but does not centralize Direct Connect connections or easily facilitate inter-Region communication over them at scale. Option D (AWS Global Accelerator) is a networking service that improves the availability and performance of applications by directing user traffic to optimal endpoints globally, but it is not designed for centralizing private network connections like Direct Connect.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_54',
  'A hybrid cloud architecture connects an on-premises data center to AWS via AWS Direct Connect. The on-premises network uses Active Directory for DNS resolution, while AWS VPCs use Amazon Route 53 private hosted zones for internal DNS. Applications running in AWS VPCs need to resolve DNS records for on-premises resources, and on-premises applications need to resolve DNS records for AWS resources. The solution must provide seamless, private, and highly available DNS resolution across both environments with minimal latency and administrative overhead. Which combination of AWS services should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configuring conditional forwarders on on-premises DNS servers to query public Route 53 resolvers.","Implementing Amazon Route 53 Resolver Endpoints in AWS VPCs and conditional forwarders on on-premises DNS servers.","Deploying custom DNS servers on Amazon EC2 instances in AWS VPCs and configuring them to forward queries.","Using public Route 53 hosted zones for all internal DNS records and updating on-premises DNS servers to query them."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Implementing Amazon Route 53 Resolver Endpoints in AWS VPCs and conditional forwarders on on-premises DNS servers) is the correct solution. Route 53 Resolver Endpoints provide a way to forward DNS queries between your VPCs and your on-premises network over a private connection like Direct Connect. An inbound endpoint allows on-premises DNS servers to query Route 53 for AWS-hosted records, and an outbound endpoint allows AWS resources to query on-premises DNS servers for on-premises records. Conditional forwarders on both sides ensure that only relevant queries are forwarded, providing seamless, private, and highly available resolution with minimal administrative overhead. Option A (Configuring conditional forwarders on on-premises DNS servers to query public Route 53 resolvers) would expose internal DNS queries to the public internet, which is a security risk and does not provide private resolution for AWS resources. Option C (Deploying custom DNS servers on Amazon EC2 instances in AWS VPCs and configuring them to forward queries) is a viable but more operationally intensive approach, requiring management of EC2 instances, patching, and scaling, which increases overhead compared to managed Route 53 Resolver Endpoints. Option D (Using public Route 53 hosted zones for all internal DNS records and updating on-premises DNS servers to query them) is a severe security risk as internal DNS records would be publicly accessible, and it does not provide private resolution for on-premises resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_55',
  'A rapidly growing startup is expanding its AWS footprint, deploying new applications in several VPCs across multiple AWS Regions. The network team is concerned about managing an increasing number of CIDR blocks, ensuring non-overlapping ranges, and simplifying routing across the expanding network. They need a strategy that allows for efficient IP address management, accommodates future growth without re-architecting, and minimizes the complexity of route tables in AWS Transit Gateway. Which CIDR allocation and routing strategy should be adopted to meet these requirements MOST effectively?',
  'mcq',
  '["Assigning /16 CIDR blocks to each new VPC regardless of actual size, and using static routes.","Utilizing a centralized IP Address Management (IPAM) solution to allocate non-overlapping, smaller CIDR blocks and leveraging Transit Gateway route summarization.","Manually assigning unique /24 CIDR blocks for each subnet and propagating all specific routes to Transit Gateway.","Relying on AWS default VPC CIDR blocks and peering all VPCs directly."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Utilizing a centralized IP Address Management (IPAM) solution to allocate non-overlapping, smaller CIDR blocks and leveraging Transit Gateway route summarization) is the most effective strategy. A centralized IPAM solution (like AWS IPAM) helps manage and allocate non-overlapping CIDR blocks efficiently, preventing IP conflicts and ensuring room for growth. Combining this with Transit Gateway route summarization significantly reduces the number of routes that need to be managed in Transit Gateway route tables, simplifying routing and improving performance, especially in large, complex networks. Option A (Assigning /16 CIDR blocks to each new VPC regardless of actual size, and using static routes) is inefficient, leading to IP address waste and potential exhaustion of the private IP space. Static routes also become unmanageable at scale. Option C (Manually assigning unique /24 CIDR blocks for each subnet and propagating all specific routes to Transit Gateway) would lead to a massive number of specific routes in Transit Gateway route tables, increasing complexity, management overhead, and potentially hitting route limits. Option D (Relying on AWS default VPC CIDR blocks and peering all VPCs directly) is not scalable, as default VPCs often have overlapping CIDR ranges, and VPC peering does not scale beyond a few connections, making it unsuitable for a rapidly growing, multi-VPC, multi-Region environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_56',
  'A company is extending its on-premises network to AWS using AWS Direct Connect. To dynamically exchange routing information between their on-premises routers and the AWS Virtual Private Gateway, which networking protocol is primarily used?',
  'mcq',
  '["Border Gateway Protocol (BGP)","Open Shortest Path First (OSPF)","Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol (EIGRP)","Routing Information Protocol (RIP)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) states Border Gateway Protocol (BGP), which is the standard routing protocol used to exchange routes between AWS Direct Connect and on-premises routers. BGP enables dynamic routing updates, ensuring that both sides of the connection have the most current routing information. Option B (OSPF), Option C (EIGRP), and Option D (RIP) are interior gateway protocols primarily used within an autonomous system or local network and are not typically used for external routing between an on-premises network and AWS Direct Connect.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_57',
  'A developer needs to ensure that traffic from a specific subnet within a VPC is routed through a Network Address Translation (NAT) Gateway for outbound internet access. Which component must be updated to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["The route table associated with the subnet","The Network ACL associated with the subnet","The security group attached to the EC2 instances","The VPC''s main route table"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the route table associated with the subnet. Each subnet in a VPC must be associated with a route table, which controls the routing for traffic leaving the subnet. To direct outbound internet traffic through a NAT Gateway, a route must be added to the subnet''s route table pointing to the NAT Gateway for internet-bound traffic (e.g., 0.0.0.0/0). Option B (Network ACLs) are stateless firewalls that control inbound and outbound traffic at the subnet level, but they do not determine routing paths. Option C (security groups) are stateful firewalls that control traffic to and from EC2 instances, not routing. Option D (the VPC''s main route table) is a default route table, but specific subnets can have custom route table associations that override the main route table.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_58',
  'A company has multiple VPCs in different AWS Regions and needs to enable full mesh connectivity between them for global application deployment. Which AWS service is designed to simplify inter-Region VPC connectivity and routing?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect Gateway","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Transit Gateway. Transit Gateway acts as a network hub that connects VPCs and on-premises networks, including across different AWS Regions, simplifying network architecture and management. It supports full mesh connectivity without requiring individual peering connections between every VPC. Option A (AWS Direct Connect Gateway) is used to connect VPCs to on-premises networks via Direct Connect, not primarily for inter-Region VPC-to-VPC communication. Option B (Amazon VPC Peering) creates a one-to-one network connection between two VPCs, which becomes complex to manage in a full-mesh scenario with many VPCs. Option D (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service and does not provide network connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_59',
  'Which statement best describes the difference between static and dynamic routing in AWS networking?',
  'mcq',
  '["Static routing requires manual route table updates, while dynamic routing automatically propagates route changes using protocols like BGP.","Static routing is used for public subnets, and dynamic routing is used for private subnets.","Static routing offers higher throughput, whereas dynamic routing provides lower latency.","Static routing is only for IPv4, while dynamic routing supports both IPv4 and IPv6."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately distinguishes between static and dynamic routing. Static routing involves manually configuring routes in a route table, which remain fixed unless manually changed. Dynamic routing, typically implemented using protocols like BGP, automatically discovers and propagates routing information, adapting to network changes without manual intervention. Option B is incorrect; both types of routing can be used in public or private subnets. Option C is incorrect; routing type does not inherently determine throughput or latency. Option D is incorrect; both static and dynamic routing can support IPv4 and IPv6.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_60',
  'A development team needs to securely connect their application running in a VPC to a third-party SaaS service hosted in another AWS account, ensuring all traffic remains within the AWS network and is not exposed to the public internet. Which AWS service should they use to achieve this with minimal operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS PrivateLink","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS Transit Gateway","An Internet Gateway with strict security group rules"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS PrivateLink. AWS PrivateLink enables private connectivity between VPCs and services hosted by other AWS accounts or AWS Marketplace partners, ensuring that traffic does not traverse the public internet. It simplifies network architecture by eliminating the need for an Internet Gateway, NAT device, or VPN connection. Option B (Amazon VPC Peering) would require the SaaS provider to peer their VPC with the customer''s VPC, which can be complex to manage for multiple customers and doesn''t inherently offer a ''service'' model. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) could connect VPCs but is more for complex multi-VPC routing within an organization, not specifically for private access to a third-party service. Option D (Internet Gateway) would expose traffic to the public internet, violating the requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_61',
  'Which of the following is a limitation of Amazon VPC Peering connections?',
  'mcq',
  '["VPC peering connections do not support transitive routing.","VPC peering connections can only be established between VPCs in the same AWS Region.","VPC peering connections cannot be used to connect VPCs in different AWS accounts.","VPC peering connections have a maximum bandwidth limit of 1 Gbps."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) highlights a key limitation: VPC peering connections do not support transitive routing. This means that if VPC A is peered with VPC B, and VPC B is peered with VPC C, traffic from VPC A cannot directly reach VPC C via VPC B. Each peering connection is a direct, one-to-one relationship. Option B is incorrect; VPC peering can be established between VPCs in different AWS Regions (inter-Region VPC peering). Option C is incorrect; VPC peering can connect VPCs in different AWS accounts. Option D is incorrect; VPC peering connections do not have a fixed bandwidth limit and scale with the underlying network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_62',
  'A network administrator wants to reduce the number of entries in route tables for a large number of subnets that share a common destination prefix. Which networking concept should they apply?',
  'mcq',
  '["Network Address Translation (NAT)","Route summarization (CIDR aggregation)","Virtual Private Network (VPN)","Elastic Network Interface (ENI)"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is route summarization, also known as CIDR aggregation. This technique allows multiple specific routes to be represented by a single, broader route entry if they share a common prefix. This reduces the size of route tables, simplifies routing decisions, and improves network efficiency. Option A (NAT) is for translating IP addresses. Option C (VPN) is for creating secure connections over a public network. Option D (ENI) is a logical networking component for EC2 instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_63',
  'What is the primary function of an AWS Direct Connect Gateway?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide a managed DNS service for hybrid cloud environments.","To enable connectivity between multiple VPCs (in the same or different Regions) and on-premises networks through a single Direct Connect connection.","To act as a firewall for all traffic entering or leaving a Direct Connect connection.","To automatically scale bandwidth for Direct Connect connections based on demand."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary function of an AWS Direct Connect Gateway. It allows you to connect your Direct Connect connection to multiple VPCs located in the same or different AWS Regions, as long as those VPCs are associated with the Direct Connect Gateway. This centralizes connectivity and simplifies management. Option A (managed DNS) describes Amazon Route 53. Option C (firewall) describes Network ACLs or security groups. Option D (automatic bandwidth scaling) is not a feature of Direct Connect Gateway; bandwidth is provisioned.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_1_64',
  'A developer is configuring a Site-to-Site VPN connection between an on-premises data center and an AWS VPC. Which routing option allows the on-premises network to dynamically learn routes from the VPC and vice-versa, without manual configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["Static routing","Dynamic routing using BGP","Default routing to an Internet Gateway","Source/Destination Check routing"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is dynamic routing using BGP. When configuring a Site-to-Site VPN connection, BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) can be enabled to dynamically exchange routing information between the on-premises customer gateway and the AWS Virtual Private Gateway. This eliminates the need for manual route table updates and ensures routes are automatically propagated. Option A (static routing) requires manual configuration of routes on both sides. Option C (default routing to an Internet Gateway) is for outbound internet access, not for VPN routing. Option D (Source/Destination Check routing) is a setting on EC2 instances, not a routing protocol for VPNs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
