  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_42',
  'Which specialized Elastic Network Adapter (ENI) type is designed for high-performance computing (HPC) and machine learning applications that require low-latency, high-throughput inter-instance communication?',
  'mcq',
  '["Standard Network Adapter.","Virtual Private Gateway.","Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA).","Elastic Network Adapter (ENA)."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA), a network interface that enables customers to run applications requiring high levels of inter-instance communication, like HPC and machine learning, at scale in AWS. Option A is a generic term. Option B is a component for VPN connections. Option D, ENA, provides enhanced networking but EFA is specialized for HPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_43',
  'What is the advantage of configuring jumbo frames (MTU of 9001) for network traffic between EC2 instances within the same Amazon VPC?',
  'mcq',
  '["Automatic encryption of all network traffic.","Reduced overhead and increased throughput for large packets.","Lower monthly AWS billing costs.","Simplified DNS resolution."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the advantage of jumbo frames. By allowing larger packets (up to 9001 bytes), jumbo frames reduce the number of packets that need to be processed, which decreases CPU overhead and can increase network throughput, especially for large data transfers. Option A is handled by encryption protocols or services. Option C is related to cost management. Option D is handled by Amazon Route 53.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_44',
  'What is the primary function of Amazon Route 53 health checks in optimizing network reliability?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store application configuration settings.","Monitor the health of endpoints and route traffic away from unhealthy ones.","Provide a managed container orchestration service.","Encrypt data at rest in databases."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary function of Amazon Route 53 health checks. They continuously monitor the health and performance of your application endpoints and can automatically route traffic away from unhealthy resources, improving reliability and availability. Option A is handled by services like AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store. Option C is handled by Amazon ECS or EKS. Option D is handled by services like Amazon RDS with AWS KMS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_45',
  'A developer wants to reduce the amount of data transferred directly from an Amazon EC2 instance to global users, thereby lowering bandwidth costs. Which AWS service can help achieve this by caching content closer to users?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect for on-premises connectivity.","Amazon SQS for message queuing.","AWS WAF for web application firewall.","Amazon CloudFront."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is Amazon CloudFront, a content delivery network (CDN) service. CloudFront caches content at edge locations globally, serving content closer to users and reducing the load and data transfer from origin servers (like EC2 instances), which helps lower bandwidth costs. Option A, AWS Direct Connect, is for dedicated on-premises connections. Option B, Amazon SQS, is a messaging service. Option C, AWS WAF, is a web application firewall.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_46',
  'When designing an Amazon VPC, what is a key consideration for selecting optimal network interfaces for EC2 instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["The color scheme of the network diagram.","The number of available IP addresses in a different AWS Region.","Matching instance type to required network performance (e.g., ENA, EFA).","The preferred programming language for application development."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is a key consideration. Selecting the appropriate network interface (like ENA for enhanced networking or EFA for HPC/ML) and ensuring the EC2 instance type supports it is crucial for meeting application network performance requirements. The other options are irrelevant to network interface selection for performance optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_47',
  'Which statement accurately describes multicast support in Amazon VPC?',
  'mcq',
  '["Multicast is fully supported natively within a standard VPC.","Multicast is supported only for IPv6 traffic.","Multicast is supported only within a Transit Gateway.","Multicast is not supported at all in Amazon VPC."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) states that multicast is supported only within a Transit Gateway in Amazon VPC. Standard VPCs do not natively support multicast traffic. This feature was introduced to allow multicast routing between attached VPCs and VPNs via the Transit Gateway. Options A, B, and D are incorrect regarding native multicast support in standard VPCs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_48',
  'A startup needs to establish a secure, high-bandwidth connection between its on-premises data center and AWS for occasional large data transfers, prioritizing cost-effectiveness over dedicated uptime. Which connection type is generally more cost-effective for this scenario compared to a dedicated line?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect for dedicated private connections.","AWS PrivateLink for private service endpoints.","Amazon VPC Peering for inter-VPC communication.","AWS Site-to-Site VPN."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is AWS Site-to-Site VPN. While AWS Direct Connect (Option A) offers dedicated high-bandwidth, it is generally more expensive due to dedicated infrastructure. For occasional large data transfers where cost-effectiveness and flexibility are prioritized over a dedicated line, a Site-to-Site VPN over the public internet is typically more cost-effective. Options B and C are for different use cases (VPC to service and VPC to VPC communication, respectively).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_49',
  'A data analytics company runs high-performance computing (HPC) workloads on Amazon EC2 instances that require extremely low-latency, high-bandwidth, and tightly coupled communication between instances. These workloads involve large datasets and frequent inter-instance data exchange, making traditional TCP/IP networking a bottleneck. The company needs to optimize network performance for these specific workloads to achieve maximum throughput and minimize processing time. Which AWS networking feature should be enabled on the EC2 instances to meet these demanding requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Jumbo Frames","Elastic Network Adapter (ENA)","Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA)","Enhanced Networking with Intel 82599 Virtual Function"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA)) is the correct answer. EFA is a network interface for Amazon EC2 instances that enables customers to run HPC and machine learning applications requiring high levels of inter-instance communication at scale. EFA provides lower and more consistent latency and higher throughput than traditional TCP networking by bypassing the operating system''s network stack and using a custom-built OS bypass capability. This is crucial for tightly coupled workloads where every microsecond of latency matters. Option A (Jumbo Frames) allows larger packet sizes (up to 9001 bytes) to reduce overhead, but it doesn''t provide the OS bypass and specialized communication protocols of EFA. Option B (Elastic Network Adapter (ENA)) provides enhanced networking, offering higher bandwidth and lower inter-instance latency than traditional EC2 networking, but it still uses the standard TCP/IP stack and does not offer the specialized HPC capabilities of EFA. Option D (Enhanced Networking with Intel 82599 Virtual Function) refers to an older generation of enhanced networking (VF) that has largely been superseded by ENA, which is the default enhanced networking for modern instance types.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_50',
  'A data processing pipeline in AWS frequently transfers large files (several gigabytes each) between Amazon EC2 instances within the same VPC. The current network configuration uses standard Ethernet frames, resulting in significant overhead due to the large number of packets required for each file transfer. The operations team wants to reduce CPU utilization on the instances and improve network throughput for these transfers by allowing larger packet sizes. Which networking feature should be enabled and configured on the EC2 instances and within the VPC to optimize these large file transfers?',
  'mcq',
  '["Jumbo Frames with an MTU of 9001 bytes.","Elastic Network Adapter (ENA) with increased bandwidth.","Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) for high-performance networking.","AWS Direct Connect for higher bandwidth."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Jumbo Frames with an MTU of 9001 bytes) is the correct solution. Jumbo frames allow an Ethernet frame to carry a payload of up to 9001 bytes, significantly larger than the standard 1500-byte MTU. For large file transfers between EC2 instances within the same VPC, using jumbo frames reduces the number of packets required, which in turn reduces CPU overhead for packet processing and increases network throughput. This requires configuring the MTU on the EC2 instances'' operating systems and ensuring the VPC''s network path supports it (which it does for intra-VPC traffic). Option B (Elastic Network Adapter (ENA) with increased bandwidth) provides enhanced networking capabilities, offering higher bandwidth and lower latency, but it doesn''t inherently change the MTU. While ENA is a prerequisite for high bandwidth, configuring jumbo frames specifically addresses the packet size optimization. Option C (Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA)) is designed for HPC and machine learning workloads requiring OS bypass and specialized communication protocols, which is overkill and not the primary solution for simply optimizing large file transfers with larger packet sizes. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) is for private connectivity between on-premises and AWS, not for optimizing intra-VPC traffic between EC2 instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_51',
  'A global software company provides a mission-critical API service hosted on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in a single AWS Region. The company needs to improve the service''s availability and performance for users worldwide, ensuring that traffic is routed to the healthiest endpoint with the lowest latency, even if there are regional internet congestion issues. The solution must provide static IP addresses for client connections and automatically fail over to a healthy endpoint in another Region if the primary Region experiences an outage. Which AWS service is best suited to meet these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53 with failover routing policies.","AWS Transit Gateway with inter-Region peering.","AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon CloudFront with multiple origins."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Global Accelerator) is the best service to meet these requirements. Global Accelerator provides static anycast IP addresses that act as fixed entry points to your application. It uses the AWS global network to route user traffic to the optimal healthy endpoint (e.g., an ALB) across AWS Regions, significantly reducing latency and improving availability by bypassing internet congestion. It automatically detects unhealthy endpoints and routes traffic to the next healthiest available endpoint, providing seamless failover. Option A (Amazon Route 53 with failover routing policies) can provide DNS-based failover and latency-based routing, but it operates at the DNS level and does not provide static IP addresses or the real-time network path optimization capabilities of Global Accelerator. Option B (AWS Transit Gateway with inter-Region peering) is for connecting VPCs and on-premises networks across Regions, not for optimizing public internet traffic from global users to application endpoints. Option D (Amazon CloudFront with multiple origins) is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) primarily for caching and accelerating content delivery, not for optimizing real-time API traffic routing based on network performance and providing static IP entry points.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_52',
  'A developer is designing an application that requires extremely high throughput for large data transfers between EC2 instances within the same VPC. To optimize network performance and reduce packet overhead, which networking feature should be enabled on the EC2 instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["Standard Ethernet frames (1500 MTU)","Jumbo frames (9001 MTU)","VPC Flow Logs","AWS PrivateLink"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is jumbo frames (9001 MTU). Jumbo frames allow for a larger Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) size, which means more data can be sent in a single packet. This reduces the number of packets required for large data transfers, thereby reducing processing overhead and increasing overall throughput, especially for traffic within the same VPC or peered VPCs. Option A (standard Ethernet frames) is the default but less efficient for large transfers. Option C (VPC Flow Logs) are for monitoring, not performance optimization. Option D (AWS PrivateLink) is for private connectivity to services, not for optimizing inter-instance throughput within a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_53',
  'A company hosts a web application on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer and wants to improve performance and availability for users globally by routing traffic through the AWS global network infrastructure. Which AWS service is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon Route 53","AWS Transit Gateway"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Global Accelerator. Global Accelerator improves the availability and performance of your applications by directing user traffic to the optimal endpoint over the AWS global network. It uses static IP addresses as fixed entry points to your applications and routes traffic to the nearest healthy endpoint, reducing latency and improving reliability. Option A (Amazon CloudFront) is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) primarily for caching static and dynamic content closer to users. Option C (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service and can perform DNS-based routing, but Global Accelerator specifically leverages the AWS global network for performance. Option D (AWS Transit Gateway) is for inter-VPC and hybrid cloud connectivity, not global user acceleration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_54',
  'A developer needs to ensure high availability for an application by automatically routing traffic away from unhealthy endpoints. Which Amazon Route 53 feature can be configured to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Simple routing policy","Latency-based routing policy","Health checks with failover routing policy","Geolocation routing policy"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is health checks with a failover routing policy. Amazon Route 53 health checks can monitor the health of your application endpoints. When combined with a failover routing policy, Route 53 automatically routes traffic to a healthy secondary endpoint if the primary endpoint fails its health checks, ensuring high availability. Option A (Simple routing policy) does not incorporate health checks. Option B (Latency-based routing policy) routes users to the endpoint with the lowest latency but doesn''t automatically failover on unhealthiness. Option D (Geolocation routing policy) routes users based on their geographic location.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_55',
  'To reduce data transfer costs and improve content delivery speed for a global audience accessing static and dynamic web content, which AWS service should a developer leverage?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon CloudFront","Amazon VPC Endpoint"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon CloudFront. CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) service that securely delivers data, videos, applications, and APIs to customers globally with low latency and high transfer speeds. It caches content at edge locations closer to users, reducing the load on origin servers and significantly lowering data transfer costs for content served from AWS. Option A (Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration) speeds up uploads to S3 buckets but doesn''t serve content globally. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) is for private connectivity between on-premises and AWS. Option D (Amazon VPC Endpoint) provides private connectivity to AWS services from within a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_56',
  'When deploying an Amazon EC2 instance that requires very high network performance for a database workload, which type of network interface is generally recommended to ensure optimal throughput and low latency?',
  'mcq',
  '["Standard Elastic Network Interface (ENI) on a T3 instance.","Elastic Network Adapter (ENA) on a current generation instance type.","A software-based VPN connection to another VPC.","A dedicated network interface with a 10 Gbps internet connection."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is an Elastic Network Adapter (ENA) on a current generation instance type. ENA provides enhanced networking capabilities, offering significantly higher packet per second (PPS) performance, lower inter-instance latency, and higher bandwidth compared to older network interfaces. This is crucial for demanding database workloads. Option A (Standard ENI on a T3 instance) might not provide the required performance for ''very high'' network demands. Option C (software-based VPN) introduces overhead and latency, making it unsuitable for optimal performance. Option D (dedicated network interface with 10 Gbps internet connection) is vague and doesn''t specify the underlying AWS technology for enhanced performance within the AWS network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_57',
  'Which AWS service allows you to enable multicast routing within a VPC, a feature often required for legacy applications or specific financial trading systems?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53","AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon VPC Peering","AWS Direct Connect"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Transit Gateway. AWS Transit Gateway supports multicast routing, allowing you to send a single stream of traffic to multiple destinations simultaneously within your VPCs or connected networks. This is a unique capability for specific use cases like media streaming or financial services that rely on multicast. Option A (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service. Option C (Amazon VPC Peering) provides unicast connectivity between two VPCs. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) provides private connectivity to on-premises networks but does not inherently enable multicast within the VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_52_m_52_3_58',
  'A company needs to establish a secure connection between their on-premises data center and an AWS VPC. They are evaluating options for cost-effectiveness. Which factor is MOST critical in determining the cost-effectiveness of an AWS Site-to-Site VPN versus AWS Direct Connect?',
  'mcq',
  '["The number of EC2 instances in the VPC.","The total volume of data transferred over the connection per month.","The number of security groups configured in the VPC.","The type of operating system running on the on-premises servers."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the total volume of data transferred over the connection per month. AWS Site-to-Site VPN charges are primarily based on connection hours, with standard data transfer out charges. AWS Direct Connect has port hour charges and lower data transfer out rates (often significantly lower for high volumes). For low data volumes, VPN is typically more cost-effective. For high, consistent data volumes, Direct Connect becomes more cost-effective due to its reduced data transfer rates, despite higher setup and fixed monthly costs. Option A (number of EC2 instances) and Option C (number of security groups) do not directly impact the cost comparison between VPN and Direct Connect. Option D (operating system) is irrelevant to network connection costs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain52_11',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_52_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

