  'Option C (An SSL/TLS certificate managed by AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) is required for an Application Load Balancer to handle HTTPS traffic and perform SSL/TLS termination. The certificate encrypts the communication between the client and the ALB. Option A is for storing logs, not for SSL/TLS termination. Option B is a serverless compute service. Option D is a NoSQL database service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_47',
  'A company requires all data flowing through its Amazon CloudFront distribution to be encrypted between the viewer and CloudFront, and between CloudFront and the origin. Which configuration ensures this end-to-end encryption?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Viewer Protocol Policy to ''HTTPS Only'' and Origin Protocol Policy to ''HTTPS Only''.","Use AWS Shield Advanced to protect against DDoS attacks.","Enable Amazon S3 server-side encryption for the origin bucket.","Implement AWS WAF with a custom rule set."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Configure Viewer Protocol Policy to ''HTTPS Only'' and Origin Protocol Policy to ''HTTPS Only'') ensures end-to-end encryption. ''Viewer Protocol Policy'' dictates how CloudFront interacts with viewers, and ''Origin Protocol Policy'' dictates how CloudFront interacts with the origin server. Option B (AWS Shield Advanced) provides DDoS protection, not encryption. Option C (Amazon S3 server-side encryption) encrypts data at rest in S3, not in transit. Option D (AWS WAF) protects against web exploits, not encryption in transit.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_48',
  'What is the primary purpose of AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide a managed service for issuing and managing public and private SSL/TLS certificates.","To encrypt data at rest within Amazon S3 buckets using customer-managed keys and server-side encryption.","To manage network access control lists (NACLs) for VPC subnets and control inbound and outbound traffic flows.","To monitor and log API calls across AWS accounts for auditing and compliance purposes."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) as a service that handles the complexity of creating, storing, and renewing SSL/TLS certificates. It integrates with other AWS services like Application Load Balancers and Amazon CloudFront to simplify certificate management for encrypted communication.
Option B (incorrect) describes a function of Amazon S3 in conjunction with AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS), not ACM. ACM does not directly encrypt data at rest.
Option C (incorrect) describes the function of Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) within Amazon VPC, which are used for subnet-level traffic filtering, unrelated to certificate management.
Option D (incorrect) describes the primary purpose of AWS CloudTrail, which records API calls for auditing and compliance, not certificate management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_49',
  'Which protocol is commonly used to establish secure, encrypted communication tunnels for data in transit over a Virtual Private Network (VPN) connection to AWS?',
  'mcq',
  '["HTTP, which is primarily used for unencrypted web traffic.","IPsec, which provides cryptographic security services for IP networks.","FTP, which is a file transfer protocol without inherent encryption.","SMTP, which is used for sending email messages securely."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is IPsec (Internet Protocol Security), a suite of protocols used to set up secure, encrypted communication between two points over an IP network. It is the standard protocol for establishing VPN tunnels to AWS, ensuring confidentiality and integrity of data in transit.
Option A (incorrect) HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is primarily used for unencrypted web traffic. While HTTPS (HTTP Secure) uses TLS for encryption, HTTP itself is not the protocol for VPN tunnels.
Option C (incorrect) FTP (File Transfer Protocol) is used for transferring files between computers and does not inherently provide encryption for VPN tunnels.
Option D (incorrect) SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) is used for sending email messages and is unrelated to establishing VPN tunnels.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_50',
  'Which security feature, when enabled for a domain hosted on Amazon Route 53, helps protect against DNS cache poisoning and other DNS-based attacks by cryptographically signing DNS records?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS WAF, which protects web applications from common web exploits.","Amazon CloudFront, a content delivery network that caches content at edge locations.","DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC), which adds cryptographic signatures to DNS records.","AWS Shield Advanced, which provides comprehensive DDoS protection for applications."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC). DNSSEC adds a layer of security to the DNS protocol by providing cryptographic authentication of DNS data, helping to prevent DNS cache poisoning and other DNS-based attacks by ensuring that DNS responses are authentic and untampered.
Option A (incorrect) AWS WAF is a web application firewall that protects web applications from common web exploits, not DNS-specific attacks.
Option B (incorrect) Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) that improves performance and security by caching content closer to users, but it does not directly protect DNS records from spoofing.
Option D (incorrect) AWS Shield Advanced provides managed Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) protection for applications, which is different from protecting the integrity of DNS records.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_51',
  'For organizations that require a custom, private certificate authority (CA) hierarchy to issue internal SSL/TLS certificates for their resources, which AWS service should be utilized?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Private Certificate Authority (ACM PCA) to create and manage private CAs.","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for public and managed private certificates without a custom CA.","AWS Organizations for managing multiple AWS accounts under a unified billing and security model.","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) for sharing AWS resources securely across accounts or within an organization."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Private Certificate Authority (ACM PCA). ACM PCA allows you to create and manage your own private CA hierarchy, enabling you to issue private certificates for internal use cases, such as authenticating internal services, users, and devices.
Option B (incorrect) AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) primarily focuses on public SSL/TLS certificates and managed private certificates, but it does not provide the capability to create and manage a custom private CA hierarchy directly.
Option C (incorrect) AWS Organizations is used for centralizing governance and management across multiple AWS accounts, not for certificate authority management.
Option D (incorrect) AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) is used for sharing AWS resources securely across AWS accounts or within your AWS Organization, which is unrelated to managing private certificate authorities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_52',
  'A company is transferring sensitive data from its on-premises data center to Amazon S3 over an AWS Direct Connect connection. The security team requires that all data in transit must be encrypted end-to-end.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon S3 bucket policies to enforce HTTPS for all uploads, which encrypts traffic over the internet.","Establish an IPsec VPN tunnel over the Direct Connect connection to add an encryption layer.","Use AWS PrivateLink to connect to Amazon S3, ensuring private connectivity without public internet exposure.","Enable server-side encryption with AWS KMS for Amazon S3 objects to encrypt data at rest."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to establish an IPsec VPN tunnel over the Direct Connect connection. While Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection, it does not inherently encrypt the data traversing the connection. An IPsec VPN tunnel adds an essential layer of end-to-end encryption for data in transit over the Direct Connect link, meeting the requirement for encrypted communication.
Option A (incorrect) Configuring S3 bucket policies to enforce HTTPS ensures encryption for data transferred to S3, but this is at the application layer and does not specifically encrypt the Direct Connect link itself for all traffic.
Option C (incorrect) AWS PrivateLink provides private connectivity to AWS services from within a VPC, avoiding the public internet. While it enhances security, it does not add an explicit encryption layer over the Direct Connect connection itself.
Option D (incorrect) Enabling server-side encryption with AWS KMS for Amazon S3 objects encrypts data at rest within S3. This addresses data security once it reaches S3 but does not encrypt data in transit over Direct Connect.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_53',
  'Which of the following best describes the primary security objective of Transport Layer Security (TLS) in network communications?',
  'mcq',
  '["To reduce the computational overhead for data transmission by optimizing packet sizes.","To ensure data compression and optimize bandwidth usage across the network.","To provide confidentiality, integrity, and authentication for data in transit over a network.","To facilitate load balancing across multiple application servers for high availability and scalability."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) accurately describes the primary security objectives of TLS. TLS (and its predecessor SSL) is designed to provide confidentiality (preventing eavesdropping), integrity (preventing tampering), and authentication (verifying identities of communicating parties) for data exchanged over a network, such as the internet.
Option A (incorrect) Reducing computational overhead is not a primary security objective of TLS; in fact, encryption adds some overhead.
Option B (incorrect) While some protocols might include compression, it is not the primary security objective of TLS. TLS focuses on securing the communication channel.
Option D (incorrect) Facilitating load balancing is a function of services like Application Load Balancers or Network Load Balancers, not the primary security objective of TLS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_54',
  'A financial services company is deploying a new web application on AWS that handles sensitive customer data. The application uses an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to distribute traffic to Amazon EC2 instances. The security team mandates that all client-to-ALB communication must enforce TLS 1.2 or higher, and certificates must be managed automatically with minimal manual intervention. The solution must ensure strong encryption while minimizing operational overhead for certificate rotation and renewal.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the ALB listener with an AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificate and enforce a security policy that requires TLS 1.2 or higher.","Install self-signed certificates on each Amazon EC2 instance and configure the ALB to use HTTPS listeners with pass-through encryption.","Use AWS Private Certificate Authority (PCA) to issue certificates and manually upload them to the ALB, then configure a custom security policy.","Deploy a third-party certificate management solution on an Amazon EC2 instance and integrate it with the ALB via a custom Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution. AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) provides managed TLS/SSL certificates that can be directly associated with an Application Load Balancer. ACM automatically handles certificate renewal and deployment, significantly minimizing operational overhead. ALB listeners can then be configured with a security policy (e.g., `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06`) that enforces TLS 1.2 or higher, meeting the encryption requirements.
Option B (incorrect) involves self-signed certificates, which are not trusted by browsers and would lead to security warnings for users. Additionally, managing certificates on individual EC2 instances for an ALB listener is not the standard or recommended approach and increases operational overhead.
Option C (incorrect) suggests using AWS Private Certificate Authority (PCA) and manually uploading certificates. While PCA can issue certificates, manually uploading them to the ALB and managing renewals defeats the purpose of minimizing operational overhead, as ACM handles this automatically for public certificates. PCA is typically used for internal PKI within an organization.
Option D (incorrect) introduces unnecessary complexity and operational overhead. Deploying a third-party solution on an EC2 instance and integrating it with Lambda for certificate management is far more complex than using ACM''s native integration with ALB, which is designed for this exact purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_55',
  'A development team is building a serverless application that uses AWS Lambda functions to process data stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. All data transferred between the Lambda functions and the S3 bucket must be encrypted in transit. The team wants to ensure this without managing any encryption keys or certificates directly. Which approach ensures data confidentiality for this communication with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the S3 bucket policy to require `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption` header for all PUT requests and ensure Lambda uses HTTPS endpoints.","Implement client-side encryption within the Lambda function before uploading objects to S3 and decrypt them upon retrieval.","Use AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) to encrypt data on the client-side within Lambda functions before sending to S3.","Establish an IPsec VPN tunnel between the Lambda execution environment and Amazon S3."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective and operationally simple method. All AWS service API calls, including those from AWS Lambda to Amazon S3, use HTTPS endpoints by default, ensuring data is encrypted in transit using TLS. Additionally, configuring an S3 bucket policy to require the `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption` header for all PUT requests ensures that objects are encrypted at rest, which is a separate but often related security requirement. This approach leverages native AWS features without additional code or infrastructure management.
Option B (incorrect) involves implementing client-side encryption within the Lambda function. While this provides strong encryption, it significantly increases development complexity and operational overhead for managing encryption/decryption logic and keys within the application code, which the team wants to avoid.
Option C (incorrect) suggests using AWS KMS for client-side encryption. Similar to Option B, this adds development complexity and requires explicit KMS API calls from the Lambda function, increasing operational overhead compared to relying on native HTTPS for in-transit encryption.
Option D (incorrect) is not a feasible or necessary solution. AWS Lambda execution environments are fully managed, and users cannot establish IPsec VPN tunnels directly from Lambda to other AWS services like Amazon S3. In-transit encryption is handled automatically via HTTPS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_56',
  'A company requires all data transferred over AWS Direct Connect connections to be encrypted end-to-end to meet stringent compliance regulations. They have an existing Direct Connect connection and are looking for the most robust and secure method to encrypt traffic between their on-premises data center and their Amazon VPCs, ensuring data confidentiality and integrity. Which solution meets this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Establish an IPsec VPN tunnel over the AWS Direct Connect connection.","Configure AWS Direct Connect Gateway with MACsec encryption.","Enable TLS encryption on all applications communicating over Direct Connect.","Utilize AWS PrivateLink to secure traffic over Direct Connect."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the standard and most robust method to achieve end-to-end encryption over an AWS Direct Connect connection. Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection, but it does not encrypt data in transit by default. By establishing an IPsec VPN tunnel over the Direct Connect connection, the traffic gains an additional layer of encryption and integrity protection, fulfilling the requirement for robust end-to-end encryption for compliance.
Option B (incorrect) refers to MACsec (Media Access Control Security), which provides encryption at Layer 2. While MACsec is supported on some Direct Connect connections, it encrypts traffic only between the customer router and the AWS Direct Connect endpoint, not end-to-end into the VPC. It also requires specific hardware support and is not as widely applicable for end-to-end encryption into a VPC as IPsec.
Option C (incorrect) suggests enabling TLS encryption on all applications. While TLS is crucial for application-layer security, relying solely on it means that any non-TLS traffic (e.g., database connections, SSH) would not be encrypted. The requirement is for end-to-end encryption for *all* data transferred over Direct Connect, which an IPsec VPN provides at the network layer.
Option D (incorrect) states utilizing AWS PrivateLink. AWS PrivateLink provides private connectivity between VPCs and services, but it does not inherently provide encryption for data in transit over a Direct Connect connection. PrivateLink ensures traffic stays within the AWS network and doesn''t traverse the public internet, but it relies on other mechanisms (like TLS at the application layer or IPsec VPN over Direct Connect) for encryption.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_57',
  'A company is hosting a public-facing web application on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). They need to ensure that all HTTP traffic is automatically redirected to HTTPS to enforce secure communication. Additionally, they want to use a custom domain name with an SSL/TLS certificate managed by AWS. Which configuration achieves this with the LEAST administrative effort?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an ALB listener on port 80 to redirect to port 443 and associate an AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificate with the ALB''s HTTPS listener.","Install and manage Let''s Encrypt certificates directly on each Amazon EC2 instance and configure NGINX to redirect HTTP to HTTPS.","Use AWS CloudFront with an S3 bucket origin, configure CloudFront to redirect HTTP to HTTPS, and use a custom SSL certificate.","Create a Network Load Balancer (NLB) with a TLS listener, upload a third-party certificate, and configure target groups for redirection."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most efficient and least administratively intensive solution. An Application Load Balancer can be configured with two listeners: one on port 80 (HTTP) with a redirect action to the HTTPS listener on port 443, and another on port 443 (HTTPS) associated with an AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificate. ACM automatically handles certificate provisioning, renewal, and deployment, significantly reducing operational overhead. This setup ensures all traffic is encrypted and redirected without manual certificate management on EC2 instances.
Option B (incorrect) involves installing and managing certificates directly on EC2 instances. While possible, this approach requires manual certificate renewal and configuration on each instance, increasing administrative effort and complexity compared to using ACM and ALB''s native features.
Option C (incorrect) describes a solution using Amazon CloudFront with an S3 bucket. While CloudFront can redirect HTTP to HTTPS and manage certificates, the scenario specifies the application is on EC2 instances behind an ALB, not static content from S3. Introducing CloudFront would add an unnecessary layer if the primary requirement is just ALB-based redirection and certificate management.
Option D (incorrect) suggests using a Network Load Balancer (NLB). While NLB supports TLS listeners, it operates at Layer 4 and does not have the native HTTP redirection capabilities of an ALB. Configuring redirection with an NLB would require additional components or complex target group logic, and uploading third-party certificates still requires manual management, unlike ACM.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_58',
  'A company stores highly confidential data in an Amazon S3 bucket. Access to this data is provided to external partners via Amazon CloudFront distributions. The security team mandates that all data in transit between CloudFront and the S3 origin must be encrypted, and the S3 bucket must only be accessible via the CloudFront distribution, preventing direct public access. Which combination of features ensures this security posture?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure CloudFront with an Origin Access Control (OAC) and ensure the S3 bucket policy grants access only to the OAC, while CloudFront uses HTTPS for origin requests.","Enable S3 bucket versioning and configure server-side encryption with S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) for the bucket.","Set up an IPsec VPN tunnel between the CloudFront edge locations and the S3 bucket.","Use CloudFront Signed URLs for access control and configure the S3 bucket to be publicly readable."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the required security posture. Origin Access Control (OAC) is the recommended method to restrict Amazon S3 bucket access to only a specific CloudFront distribution, preventing direct public access to the bucket. When configuring the CloudFront distribution, setting the ''Viewer Protocol Policy'' to ''Redirect HTTP to HTTPS'' or ''HTTPS Only'' ensures client-to-CloudFront encryption, and CloudFront automatically uses HTTPS for requests to S3 origins, encrypting data in transit between CloudFront and S3. This combination ensures both restricted access and in-transit encryption.
Option B (incorrect) focuses on S3 bucket versioning and server-side encryption at rest (SSE-S3). While important for data durability and encryption at rest, these features do not address the requirement for in-transit encryption between CloudFront and S3, nor do they restrict direct access to the S3 bucket from outside CloudFront.
Option C (incorrect) suggests an IPsec VPN tunnel. CloudFront edge locations are managed by AWS, and users cannot establish IPsec VPN tunnels from CloudFront to an S3 bucket. This is not a feasible or supported architecture for securing CloudFront-to-S3 communication.
Option D (incorrect) proposes using CloudFront Signed URLs for access control. While Signed URLs are excellent for fine-grained, time-limited access, configuring the S3 bucket to be publicly readable directly contradicts the requirement to prevent direct public access to the S3 bucket. This would expose the data if the Signed URLs were bypassed or improperly configured.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_59',
  'A development team is building a new microservices application where services communicate with each other using Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda. The security team mandates that all API calls must be encrypted in transit and authenticated using standard industry practices. The team wants to ensure TLS 1.2 or higher is enforced for all client-to-API Gateway communication. Which configuration ensures this with the LEAST configuration effort?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a custom domain name for API Gateway, associate an AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificate, and set the minimum TLS version to 1.2.","Implement a custom authorizer Lambda function to validate TLS versions on incoming requests to API Gateway.","Deploy a Network Load Balancer (NLB) in front of API Gateway and configure its listener to enforce TLS 1.2.","Use client-side certificates for mutual TLS authentication and configure API Gateway to require them."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most straightforward and least effort solution. When using a custom domain name with Amazon API Gateway, you can associate an AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificate. API Gateway then allows you to configure a ''minimum TLS version'' for the custom domain, which can be set to TLS 1.2 or higher. This automatically enforces the desired TLS version for all client-to-API Gateway communication without requiring any custom code or additional infrastructure, as ACM handles certificate management and API Gateway handles the TLS policy enforcement.
Option B (incorrect) suggests implementing a custom authorizer Lambda function to validate TLS versions. While custom authorizers can perform authentication and authorization, inspecting and enforcing TLS versions at the application layer within a Lambda function is overly complex and inefficient. API Gateway''s native TLS configuration is designed for this purpose.
Option C (incorrect) proposes deploying a Network Load Balancer (NLB) in front of API Gateway. This adds significant architectural complexity and cost without providing a direct benefit for enforcing TLS versions on API Gateway, which has its own built-in capabilities for this. NLBs are typically used for TCP/UDP traffic, not for managing API Gateway''s HTTP/HTTPS endpoint TLS policies.
Option D (incorrect) involves using client-side certificates for mutual TLS (mTLS). While mTLS provides strong authentication, it is a more advanced security measure primarily focused on client identity verification, not just enforcing a minimum TLS version for general encryption. It also adds significant complexity for client-side certificate distribution and management, which is beyond the ''least configuration effort'' constraint for simply enforcing TLS 1.2+.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_60',
  'A company is developing a new application that uses Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. The security team requires that all connections to the database instance must be encrypted in transit using SSL/TLS. The development team needs to ensure this enforcement while minimizing the impact on application code changes and operational management. Which approach meets these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the Amazon RDS instance to require SSL connections by setting the `rds.force_ssl` parameter to `1` in the parameter group.","Implement client-side encryption within the application code to encrypt all data before sending it to the RDS instance.","Deploy an AWS Network Firewall in front of the RDS instance and configure it to allow only SSL/TLS traffic.","Establish an IPsec VPN tunnel between the application servers and the Amazon RDS instance."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective and operationally simple method. Amazon RDS allows you to enforce SSL/TLS for all connections by modifying the database parameter group associated with the instance. For PostgreSQL, setting the `rds.force_ssl` parameter to `1` ensures that the database server will only accept connections that use SSL/TLS. This is a server-side configuration that requires minimal application code changes (applications just need to be configured to use SSL, which is standard for database drivers) and no additional infrastructure, directly meeting the in-transit encryption requirement.
Option B (incorrect) suggests implementing client-side encryption within the application code. While this provides encryption, it''s typically for encryption at rest or end-to-end application-layer encryption, not for enforcing in-transit network encryption. It also significantly increases development complexity and application code changes, which the team wants to minimize.
Option C (incorrect) proposes deploying an AWS Network Firewall in front of the RDS instance. AWS Network Firewall operates at the VPC level and can filter traffic based on protocols and ports, but it cannot enforce SSL/TLS encryption for database connections at the application layer. RDS instances are typically accessed within a private VPC, and a firewall would not directly enforce the SSL requirement for the database protocol itself.
Option D (incorrect) involves establishing an IPsec VPN tunnel. While VPNs provide encrypted tunnels, they are typically used for connecting on-premises networks to VPCs or between VPCs. For connections within the same VPC or from EC2 instances to RDS, relying on the database''s native SSL/TLS enforcement is much simpler and more efficient than setting up a separate VPN tunnel.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_61',
  'A media company uses Amazon S3 to store large video files and distributes them globally via Amazon CloudFront. They need to ensure that all content served to viewers is encrypted in transit and that only specific users with valid credentials can access premium content. The solution must provide a seamless user experience and minimize the management of access keys. Which approach effectively combines in-transit encryption and secure content delivery?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure CloudFront with an HTTPS viewer protocol policy and use CloudFront Signed URLs or Signed Cookies for authenticated access to the premium content.","Enable S3 bucket encryption with AWS KMS and configure CloudFront to use an Origin Access Control (OAC) to restrict S3 access.","Implement client-side encryption for video files before uploading them to S3 and distribute decryption keys to authorized users.","Establish an IPsec VPN tunnel between the viewer''s device and the CloudFront edge location for encrypted communication."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Configuring CloudFront with an HTTPS viewer protocol policy (e.g., ''Redirect HTTP to HTTPS'' or ''HTTPS Only'') ensures that all communication between viewers and CloudFront edge locations is encrypted in transit using TLS. For authenticated access to premium content, CloudFront Signed URLs or Signed Cookies are the standard and most effective mechanisms. They allow you to grant temporary, time-limited access to specific content based on user authentication, providing a seamless user experience and minimizing the management of access keys, as access is controlled at the CloudFront level.
Option B (incorrect) focuses on S3 bucket encryption with AWS KMS (encryption at rest) and Origin Access Control (OAC) to restrict S3 access. While OAC is crucial for securing the S3 origin from direct access, and KMS provides encryption at rest, these do not directly address the requirement for authenticated access to *premium content* via CloudFront or ensure in-transit encryption from CloudFront to the viewer. The viewer protocol policy handles client-to-CloudFront encryption.
Option C (incorrect) suggests client-side encryption for video files and distributing decryption keys. This approach significantly increases complexity for both the content provider (managing encryption/decryption, key distribution) and the end-users (requiring specific decryption software or steps), leading to a poor user experience and high operational overhead.
Option D (incorrect) proposes establishing an IPsec VPN tunnel between the viewer''s device and the CloudFront edge location. This is not a practical or scalable solution for content delivery to a large number of global viewers. CloudFront is designed to deliver content over the public internet securely using HTTPS, not via individual VPN tunnels for each viewer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_62',
  'A company is migrating its on-premises application to AWS. The application requires secure communication between Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet and an Amazon S3 bucket for storing application logs. The security team mandates that all traffic must remain within the AWS network and be encrypted in transit, without traversing the public internet. Which solution provides the MOST secure and efficient communication path?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an Amazon S3 VPC Endpoint (Gateway type) and ensure that all S3 API calls from EC2 instances use HTTPS.","Establish an IPsec VPN tunnel between the EC2 instances and the Amazon S3 service endpoint.","Route all S3 traffic through a NAT Gateway in a public subnet, ensuring HTTPS is used.","Use an Internet Gateway for S3 access and rely on S3 bucket policies to restrict access."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most secure and efficient solution. An Amazon S3 VPC Endpoint (Gateway type) allows Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet to access Amazon S3 directly, without requiring an Internet Gateway, NAT Gateway, or VPN connection. This ensures that traffic remains entirely within the AWS network. All communication with S3 endpoints, including those via VPC Endpoints, uses HTTPS by default, providing in-transit encryption. This setup meets both the ''within AWS network'' and ''encrypted in transit'' requirements with minimal configuration.
Option B (incorrect) suggests establishing an IPsec VPN tunnel between EC2 instances and the S3 service endpoint. This is not a standard or supported method for connecting to AWS services like S3 from within a VPC. VPC Endpoints are designed for this purpose.
Option C (incorrect) involves routing S3 traffic through a NAT Gateway. While a NAT Gateway allows instances in a private subnet to connect to S3 (which is a public service), it introduces an additional hop and potentially traverses public internet infrastructure (even if within AWS''s backbone) before reaching S3. It also adds cost and is less direct than a VPC Endpoint for S3.
Option D (incorrect) proposes using an Internet Gateway for S3 access. This would expose the private subnet instances to the public internet, which violates the requirement that traffic must remain within the AWS network and not traverse the public internet. While S3 bucket policies can restrict access, the traffic path itself would not be private.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_63',
  'A company requires all internal applications to use certificates issued by their private Certificate Authority (CA) for mutual TLS authentication. They want to integrate this private CA with AWS services to issue and manage certificates for their internal Application Load Balancers (ALBs) and other internal resources. Which AWS service is designed to help manage and issue private certificates within AWS, integrating with existing CAs?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Private Certificate Authority (PCA)","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the correct service. AWS Private Certificate Authority (PCA) allows you to create your own private CA hierarchy and issue private certificates for internal use. It integrates with AWS services like Application Load Balancers (ALBs) for internal load balancers, and it can be configured to subordinate to an existing on-premises CA, fulfilling the requirement to integrate with their private CA. PCA manages the lifecycle of these private certificates, including issuance and revocation.
Option B (incorrect) refers to AWS Certificate Manager (ACM). ACM manages public SSL/TLS certificates and private certificates issued by ACM PCA. While ACM integrates with ALBs for public certificates and can import private certificates, ACM itself does not act as a private CA for issuing certificates from an existing on-premises CA hierarchy. It primarily manages the lifecycle of certificates.
Option C (incorrect) is AWS Key Management Service (KMS). KMS is used for creating and managing cryptographic keys. While certificates rely on cryptographic keys, KMS itself is not a certificate authority and does not issue or manage certificates. It can be used to protect the private keys associated with certificates.
Option D (incorrect) is AWS Secrets Manager. Secrets Manager is used for storing and managing secrets such as database credentials, API keys, and other sensitive information. It is not a service for issuing or managing SSL/TLS certificates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_64',
  'A development team is deploying a new containerized application on Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) using AWS Fargate. The application needs to access an Amazon DynamoDB table. The security team mandates that all traffic between the ECS tasks and DynamoDB must remain within the AWS network and never traverse the public internet. Which solution ensures this secure and private connectivity?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create an Amazon DynamoDB VPC Endpoint (Interface type) in the VPC where the ECS tasks are running.","Configure an Internet Gateway for the VPC and ensure the ECS tasks use HTTPS to connect to DynamoDB.","Establish an IPsec VPN tunnel between the ECS tasks and the DynamoDB service endpoint.","Route all DynamoDB traffic through a NAT Gateway in a public subnet."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution. An Amazon DynamoDB VPC Endpoint (Interface type) allows Amazon ECS tasks (and other resources within the VPC) to connect to DynamoDB directly and privately, without traversing the public internet. Interface VPC Endpoints are powered by AWS PrivateLink, ensuring that all traffic between the ECS tasks and DynamoDB remains within the AWS network. This meets the security requirement for private and secure connectivity.
Option B (incorrect) suggests configuring an Internet Gateway. While ECS tasks can access DynamoDB over the internet using HTTPS, this violates the requirement that traffic must *never traverse the public internet*. An Internet Gateway provides public internet access, which is precisely what the requirement aims to avoid.
Option C (incorrect) proposes establishing an IPsec VPN tunnel between ECS tasks and DynamoDB. This is not a supported or practical method for connecting to AWS services like DynamoDB from within a VPC. VPC Endpoints are the designed solution for private connectivity to AWS services.
Option D (incorrect) involves routing DynamoDB traffic through a NAT Gateway. A NAT Gateway allows instances in a private subnet to initiate outbound connections to the internet, including public AWS service endpoints like DynamoDB. However, this traffic still technically traverses the public AWS network infrastructure (even if it doesn''t leave AWS''s backbone to the broader internet) and is not as private or direct as using a VPC Endpoint, which keeps traffic entirely within the private IP space of the VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_65',
  'A developer is building a web application that requires secure HTTPS communication. Which AWS service is primarily used to provision, manage, and deploy SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services like Elastic Load Balancing and Amazon CloudFront?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Certificate Manager) is the correct service for provisioning, managing, and deploying SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services. It handles the complexity of creating and renewing certificates, ensuring secure HTTPS communication. Option B (AWS Key Management Service) is used for creating and managing encryption keys, not SSL/TLS certificates themselves. Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) is for securely storing and rotating database credentials, API keys, and other secrets. Option D (AWS Identity and Access Management) is for managing access to AWS services and resources, not for certificate management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_66',
  'A company needs to establish a secure, encrypted connection between its on-premises data center and its Amazon VPC. Which protocol is commonly used with AWS Site-to-Site VPN to ensure confidentiality of data in transit?',
  'mcq',
  '["HTTP/2","IPsec","FTP","UDP"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (IPsec) is the correct protocol commonly used with AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections to provide secure, encrypted communication between on-premises networks and Amazon VPCs. IPsec ensures confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity of data in transit. Option A (HTTP/2) is a protocol for web communication, not for VPN tunnels. Option C (FTP) is a file transfer protocol and is not used for securing VPN connections. Option D (UDP) is a connectionless transport layer protocol and does not inherently provide encryption or security for VPN tunnels.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_67',
  'A developer has deployed a static website on Amazon S3 and is using Amazon CloudFront to distribute its content globally. To ensure all user traffic to the website is encrypted, which CloudFront configuration should be applied?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the Origin Protocol Policy to HTTP only","Set up a custom SSL certificate on the S3 bucket","Configure the Viewer Protocol Policy to HTTPS Only","Enable server-side encryption on the S3 bucket"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Configure the Viewer Protocol Policy to HTTPS Only) is the correct setting to ensure all user traffic to the CloudFront distribution is encrypted. This policy forces viewers to connect to CloudFront using HTTPS. Option A (Configure the Origin Protocol Policy to HTTP only) would allow unencrypted communication between CloudFront and the S3 origin, which doesn''t ensure end-to-end encryption for the viewer. Option B (Set up a custom SSL certificate on the S3 bucket) is not a direct CloudFront configuration and S3 buckets do not directly support custom SSL certificates for public access in the same way CloudFront does. Option D (Enable server-side encryption on the S3 bucket) encrypts data at rest within S3, but it does not encrypt data in transit between the viewer and CloudFront or between CloudFront and S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_68',
  'Which AWS service helps developers secure their applications by providing a managed service for creating, storing, and renewing SSL/TLS certificates that can be integrated with services like Elastic Load Balancing and Amazon CloudFront?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) is the correct service for provisioning, managing, and deploying SSL/TLS certificates. It simplifies the process of securing network communications by handling certificate lifecycle management. Option A (AWS Secrets Manager) is used for managing database credentials, API keys, and other secrets. Option B (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)) is for creating and controlling encryption keys. Option C (AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)) is for managing access to AWS resources and services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_69',
  'A developer is deploying a new microservice on AWS Lambda and needs to ensure that all communication between the Lambda function and an Amazon RDS database instance is encrypted in transit. What is the most straightforward way to achieve this for the database connection?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the database client in the Lambda function to use SSL/TLS for connections.","Use AWS Direct Connect to establish a private, encrypted connection to the RDS instance.","Enable server-side encryption on the Amazon RDS database instance.","Deploy a custom VPN tunnel between the Lambda execution environment and the RDS instance."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Configure the database client in the Lambda function to use SSL/TLS for connections) is the most straightforward and standard way to encrypt data in transit between a Lambda function and an Amazon RDS instance. RDS supports SSL/TLS connections, and the client application (Lambda function) needs to be configured to use it. Option B (Use AWS Direct Connect) is for private connections between on-premises and AWS, not typically for inter-service communication within AWS. Option C (Enable server-side encryption on the Amazon RDS database instance) encrypts data at rest, not data in transit. Option D (Deploy a custom VPN tunnel) is overly complex and unnecessary for inter-service communication within AWS, as native SSL/TLS support is available.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_3_70',
  'A development team uses an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to distribute traffic to their web application. They need to ensure that the SSL/TLS certificates used by the ALB are automatically renewed and managed with minimal operational overhead. Which AWS service should they integrate with their ALB for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS CloudFormation"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) is the correct service to integrate with an ALB for automatic SSL/TLS certificate renewal and management with minimal operational overhead. ACM handles the entire lifecycle of public and private certificates. Option A (AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)) is for managing encryption keys, not SSL/TLS certificates. Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing application secrets like database credentials. Option D (AWS CloudFormation) is an infrastructure as code service, which can deploy resources that use ACM, but it doesn''t manage certificate renewal itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_3',
  'medium'
);

