INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_1_102',
  'A financial institution is deploying a critical application in AWS that requires stringent network security, including intrusion prevention and deep packet inspection for all traffic entering and leaving its VPC. They need a managed firewall service that can enforce granular traffic filtering rules. Order the steps to implement AWS Network Firewall for a VPC.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Firewall Policy and define stateful and stateless rule groups.","Create an AWS Network Firewall endpoint in each Availability Zone of the VPC.","Update the VPC route tables to direct traffic through the Network Firewall endpoints.","Associate the Firewall Policy with the Network Firewall."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'Implementing AWS Network Firewall requires a specific sequence of configuration steps. Option A (Create a Firewall Policy and define stateful and stateless rule groups) is the initial step to define the security rules. Option B (Create an AWS Network Firewall endpoint in each Availability Zone of the VPC) deploys the firewall infrastructure within your VPC. Option D (Associate the Firewall Policy with the Network Firewall) links your defined rules to the deployed firewall. Finally, Option C (Update the VPC route tables to direct traffic through the Network Firewall endpoints) is crucial to ensure that all relevant traffic flows through the firewall for inspection and filtering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_1_103',
  'A development team needs to ensure that only specific backend services running on Amazon EC2 instances can access a sensitive Amazon RDS database within the same VPC. All other traffic must be explicitly denied to the database. Order the steps to achieve this using native VPC security features.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a security group for the Amazon RDS database instance, allowing inbound traffic only from the EC2 security group.","Launch the Amazon RDS database instance into a private subnet.","Launch the Amazon EC2 instances into a private subnet and assign them to a specific security group.","Verify connectivity from the EC2 instances to the RDS database and confirm other traffic is blocked."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'Securing access between EC2 and RDS within a VPC requires careful configuration of security groups. Option B (Launch the Amazon RDS database instance into a private subnet) is the first step, ensuring the database is not publicly accessible. Option C (Launch the Amazon EC2 instances into a private subnet and assign them to a specific security group) sets up the application tier. Option A (Create a security group for the Amazon RDS database instance, allowing inbound traffic only from the EC2 security group) is the critical step to restrict database access. Finally, Option D (Verify connectivity from the EC2 instances to the RDS database and confirm other traffic is blocked) is essential to validate the security configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_1_104',
  'An organization is building a public-facing web application that needs protection against common web exploits and bot traffic, while also ensuring high availability. They decide to use AWS WAF to protect their Application Load Balancer. Order the steps to deploy and configure AWS WAF for an ALB.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Application Load Balancer (ALB) and configure listeners for the web application.","Create a Web Access Control List (Web ACL) in AWS WAF.","Add rules to the Web ACL to block common web exploits and unwanted traffic patterns.","Associate the Web ACL with the Application Load Balancer."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Deploying AWS WAF to protect an ALB follows a logical sequence. Option A (Create an Application Load Balancer (ALB) and configure listeners for the web application) is the prerequisite, as WAF protects resources like ALBs. Option B (Create a Web Access Control List (Web ACL) in AWS WAF) is the next step, defining the container for your security rules. Option C (Add rules to the Web ACL to block common web exploits and unwanted traffic patterns) populates the Web ACL with the actual protection logic. Finally, Option D (Associate the Web ACL with the Application Load Balancer) links the configured WAF protection to the ALB, enabling it to filter incoming requests.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_1_105',
  'A security architect needs to implement a multi-tier web application architecture within an Amazon VPC, ensuring strict network segmentation between the public-facing web tier, the application tier, and the database tier. Order the steps to configure network access control using security groups and Network ACLs.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create security groups for each tier (web, application, database) with granular inbound/outbound rules.","Create a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with public and private subnets across multiple Availability Zones.","Deploy EC2 instances for the web and application tiers, and an RDS instance for the database tier, assigning appropriate security groups.","Configure Network ACLs for each subnet to provide a stateless, coarse-grained layer of network security."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'Building a secure multi-tier architecture in a VPC requires a structured approach. Option B (Create a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with public and private subnets across multiple Availability Zones) is the foundational step, defining the network environment. Option A (Create security groups for each tier (web, application, database) with granular inbound/outbound rules) defines the instance-level security policies. Option D (Configure Network ACLs for each subnet to provide a stateless, coarse-grained layer of network security) adds a subnet-level security layer. Finally, Option C (Deploy EC2 instances for the web and application tiers, and an RDS instance for the database tier, assigning appropriate security groups) places the resources within this secured network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_1_106',
  'An organization needs to protect its Amazon CloudFront distributions, Application Load Balancers, and Amazon Route 53 hosted zones from sophisticated DDoS attacks, including volumetric and application-layer attacks. They require enhanced protection and cost protection against large-scale attacks. Order the steps to enable AWS Shield Advanced for these resources.',
  'ordering',
  '["Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced.","Select the AWS resources (CloudFront distributions, ALBs, Route 53 hosted zones) to protect.","Configure custom health checks and alarms for protected resources to enable proactive DDoS response.","Review and validate the protection status and incident reports in the AWS Shield console."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Enabling AWS Shield Advanced involves a clear sequence to ensure comprehensive DDoS protection. Option A (Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced) is the necessary first step to gain access to the service''s enhanced features. Option B (Select the AWS resources (CloudFront distributions, ALBs, Route 53 hosted zones) to protect) then specifies which assets will benefit from Shield Advanced. Option C (Configure custom health checks and alarms for protected resources to enable proactive DDoS response) allows Shield Advanced to more effectively detect and mitigate attacks by understanding your application''s health. Finally, Option D (Review and validate the protection status and incident reports in the AWS Shield console) is crucial for monitoring and ensuring the effectiveness of the protection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_1_107',
  'A security engineer needs to restrict access to an Amazon S3 bucket from specific Amazon EC2 instances within a VPC, ensuring that only authorized instances can perform S3 operations. This requires a combination of VPC endpoints and S3 bucket policies. Order the steps to implement this secure access pattern.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 (Gateway type) in the VPC.","Configure the S3 bucket policy to allow access only from the VPC endpoint and specific EC2 instance IDs or VPC endpoint IDs.","Ensure the EC2 instances have an IAM role with permissions to access Amazon S3.","Verify that EC2 instances can access S3 via the endpoint and unauthorized access is denied."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'To restrict S3 access from specific EC2 instances via a VPC endpoint, the configuration must be precise. Option A (Create a VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 (Gateway type) in the VPC) is the first step, establishing the private connection to S3. Option C (Ensure the EC2 instances have an IAM role with permissions to access Amazon S3) provides the necessary permissions for the instances. Option B (Configure the S3 bucket policy to allow access only from the VPC endpoint and specific EC2 instance IDs or VPC endpoint IDs) is the critical step to enforce granular access control at the bucket level. Finally, Option D (Verify that EC2 instances can access S3 via the endpoint and unauthorized access is denied) confirms the security posture.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_1_108',
  'A security team needs to implement a perimeter VPC strategy, where all inbound and outbound internet traffic for multiple application VPCs is routed through a central inspection VPC. This central VPC will host network security appliances. Order the steps to establish this traffic flow using AWS Transit Gateway.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Transit Gateway and attach it to the inspection VPC and all application VPCs.","Configure route tables in the application VPCs to send all internet-bound traffic to the Transit Gateway.","Deploy network security appliances (e.g., firewalls) in the inspection VPC.","Configure Transit Gateway route tables to direct internet-bound traffic from application VPCs to the inspection VPC, and from the inspection VPC to an Internet Gateway."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}',
  'Implementing a perimeter VPC with Transit Gateway requires careful routing configuration. Option A (Create an AWS Transit Gateway and attach it to the inspection VPC and all application VPCs) is the foundational step, connecting all VPCs. Option C (Deploy network security appliances (e.g., firewalls) in the inspection VPC) places the security controls. Option D (Configure Transit Gateway route tables to direct internet-bound traffic from application VPCs to the inspection VPC, and from the inspection VPC to an Internet Gateway) is crucial for routing traffic through the inspection VPC. Finally, Option B (Configure route tables in the application VPCs to send all internet-bound traffic to the Transit Gateway) ensures that application VPC traffic uses the Transit Gateway for internet access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_53_m_53_1_109',
  'A security team needs to implement a robust threat modeling process for a new application being developed on AWS. This process aims to identify potential security vulnerabilities early in the development lifecycle. Order the typical steps involved in conducting a threat model for an AWS application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify potential threats and vulnerabilities to the application and its AWS components.","Define the scope of the application and its AWS architecture, including data flows and trust boundaries.","Mitigate identified threats by implementing security controls and architectural changes.","Document the threat model findings, including threats, risks, and mitigation strategies."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Threat modeling is a structured process to identify and mitigate security risks. Option B (Define the scope of the application and its AWS architecture, including data flows and trust boundaries) is the essential first step to understand what is being protected. Option A (Identify potential threats and vulnerabilities to the application and its AWS components) follows, as you can only find threats once the scope is clear. Option C (Mitigate identified threats by implementing security controls and architectural changes) is the action phase, addressing the discovered vulnerabilities. Finally, Option D (Document the threat model findings, including threats, risks, and mitigation strategies) is crucial for record-keeping, compliance, and future reference.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain53_11',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_53_1',
  'hard'
);

